\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Planned Parenthood files lawsuit against Texas\u8217\'20\'19s extreme  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ban\b0\par\par\uc2 Mary Tuma in Austin \par \uc2 788 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 July 2021\par 15:40 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Groups in the lawsuit, including doctors, health clinic staff and clergy members, say SB8 violates Texans\u8217\'20\'19 constitutional rights\par \par \uc2 In a highly anticipated move, Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://reproductiverights.org/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/WWH-v.-Jackson-Complaint.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filed a federal lawsuit}}}\uc2  on Tuesday against the state\u8217\'20\'19s draconian six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, Senate Bill 8, which would empower private citizens to enforce the law.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Texas legislature\u8217\'20\'19s well-documented hostility to the rights of pregnant people has gone to a new extreme,\u8221\'20\'1D reads the lawsuit, filed by groups including Whole Woman\u8217\'20\'19s Health and Planned Parenthood, along with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  support fund groups, doctors, health clinic staff and clergy members. \u8220\'20\'1CSenate Bill 8 flagrantly violates the constitutional rights of Texans seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and upends the rule of law in service of an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  agenda.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The extreme anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law, which offers no exception for rape or incest, bars the procedure when embryonic cardiac activity is detected, which is around six weeks \u8211\'20\'13 before most women even know they are pregnant \u8211\'20\'13 amounting to a near-total ban. It also gives any private citizen, including anti-choice activists unrelated to the patient, the right to sue an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider, rather than tasking the state with enforcement.\par \par \uc2 In fact, any person that \u8220\'20\'1Caids or abets\u8221\'20\'1D \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, such as a friend or family member that pays for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or a sexual assault counselor who calls a clinic on behalf of a patient, could be targeted, potentially opening the floodgates to harassing lawsuits that could push \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics into forced closure.\par \par \uc2 Although \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients cannot be sued under SB8, any \u8220\'20\'1Cabusive partner, controlling parent, or disapproving neighbor\u8221\'20\'1D could go after the patient\u8217\'20\'19s doctor in court, the lawsuit notes. While those who sue can collect a minimum of $10,000 if they are successful, those unjustly sued cannot recover legal fees.\par \par \uc2 Courts have halted so-called \u8220\'20\'1Cheartbeat\u8221\'20\'1D laws in a dozen other states, but the private enforcement provision in SB8 is the first of its kind in the US.\par \par \uc2 The law violates Texans\u8217\'20\'19 constitutional right to privacy and liberty as established by Roe v Wade and the right to free speech and equal protection for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, the lawsuit claims.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is unthinkable that anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  extremists could be allowed to stand in the way of people accessing essential healthcare,\u8221\'20\'1D said Melaney A Linton, president of Planned Parenthood Gulf Coast.\par \par \uc2 The GOP-dominated Texas legislature ushered through SB8 earlier this year and the Republican governor, Greg Abbott, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/may/19/texas-abortion-ban-law-greg-abbott"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 signed it into law}}}\uc2  in May. Pro-choice attorneys vowed to file suit against SB8 before it is scheduled to go into effect on 1 September. Plaintiffs are represented by several legal groups including the \uc2 Center for Reproductive Rights\uc2  (CRR), \uc2 Planned Parenthood Federation of America\uc2 , and \uc2 the American Civil Liberties Union\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve never seen a law like this,\u8221\'20\'1D said Marc Hearron, lead attorney with CRR, during a press call on Tuesday. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is what Texas politicians wanted when they passed this law: to turn anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  extremists into vigilantes with the power to police clinics, staff and their patient support systems. We have already seen the threats begin.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Due to the unprecedented private cause of action clause in SB8, which seeks to largely shield the state from enforcement of the law, the suit takes aim at every state trial court judge and county clerk in Texas who would be called upon to enforce the ban, as well as the Texas Medical Board, Texas Board of Pharmacy, the Texas Board of Nursing and the attorney general.\par \par \uc2 The suit also names Mark Lee Dickson, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activist with Right to Life of East Texas, as a defendant for his role in encouraging people to sue their local \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers under the law. Dickson {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/apr/27/us-tiny-towns-anti-abortion-ordinances"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 helped spearhead}}}\uc2  the \u8220\'20\'1Csanctuary city for the unborn\u8221\'20\'1D movement, resulting in nearly two dozen Texas towns (as well as cities in Ohio and Nebraska) adopting local ordinances that ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Similar to SB8, the local ordinances include a private enforcement provision, albeit far narrower in scope. In June, a federal judge {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2021/06/01/abortion-planned-parenthood-lubbock/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 dismissed}}}\uc2  a recent legal challenge to the ordinance in Lubbock (the most populous town to pass the local rule thus far), ruling that Planned Parenthood did not have standing to sue the city. As a result, the reproductive health clinic halted its \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services.\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://sites.utexas.edu/txpep/files/2021/07/TxPEP-research-brief-senate-bill-8.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 recent research brief}}}\uc2  from the Texas Policy Evaluation Project showed that SB8 would prevent the majority of pregnant people (eight in 10) in Texas from obtaining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, and would disproportionately affect low-income people of color.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf permitted to take effect, SB8 will create absolute chaos in Texas and irreparably harm Texans in need of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services,\u8221\'20\'1D the lawsuit reads.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210714eh7e00209\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
