\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Covid \u8216\'20\'18remains a serious and deadly threat\u8217\'20\'19 for unvaccinated people, Biden says \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Maanvi Singh in San Francisco (now) and Joan E Greve in Washington (earlier) \par \uc2 8,160 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 June 2021\par 16:24 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 President hails 300m vaccine doses administered since JanuaryMike Pence heckled at conservative Christian summit in FloridaMcConnell says voting rights proposal is \u8216\'20\'18rotten to the core\u8217\'20\'19The inner circle egging on Trump and fueling his election lie\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.00am BST\par \par \uc2 Summary\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  warned that coronavirus \u8220\'20\'1Cremains a serious and deadly threat\u8221\'20\'1D for unvaccinated Americans. In a speech celebrating 300 million vaccine doses being administered since he took office in January, the president pleaded with unvaccinated Americans to get their shots as quickly as possible. \u8220\'20\'1CDeaths and hospitalizations are drastically down in places where people are getting vaccinated,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden said. \u8220\'20\'1CBut unfortunately, cases and hospitalizations are not going down in many places in the lower vaccination rate states. They\u8217\'20\'19re actually going up in some places.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 * Many Americans are resuming pre-pandemic activities, even as they worry that restrictions were lifted too quickly, according to a poll from the Associated Press and NORC Center for Public Affairs Research. More than a third of those polled said restrictions in their area were lifted too quickly, and 27% said they weren\u8217\'20\'19t lifted quickly enough. Americans were increasingly seeing family, shopping for non-essential items, and going to bars and restaurants in June, compared to May.\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  was heckled as a \u8220\'20\'1Ctraitor\u8221\'20\'1D as he addressed a religious conservative conference in Orlando. The former vice-president received a standing ovation as he took the stage, but a small group of attendees, who were quickly escorted out, shouted insults at Pence as he started speaking. Pence has received criticism from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and some of his most ardent supporters for not attempting to overturn the results of the presidential election.\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  visited Atlanta, Georgia, as part of the Biden administration\u8217\'20\'19s nationwide tour to encourage more Americans to get vaccinated. Visiting a pop-up vaccination site at Ebenezer Baptist Church this afternoon, the vice-president thanked those present for getting their shots. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat you are doing truly is about leadership. These vaccines are safe and effective. It will save your life and lives of people that you love,\u8221\'20\'1D Harris said.\par \par \uc2 * Republicans pushed back against Joe Manchin\u8217\'20\'19s compromise proposal on a voting rights bill, which the Democratic senator laid out in a memo to colleagues this week. Manchin\u8217\'20\'19s bill would include Republican demands like requiring voter ID and allowing controversial purges of voter rolls, but Senate minority leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  said the proposal was still \u8220\'20\'1Can assault on the fundamental idea that states, not the federal government, should decide how to run their own elections\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 * Biden released a proclamation recognizing Juneteenth, which commemorates the end of slavery in America. Biden signed a bill yesterday to make Juneteenth, which falls on June 19, a federal holiday. \u8220\'20\'1CJuneteenth is a day of profound weight and power,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden said in his proclamation. \u8220\'20\'1COn Juneteenth, we recommit ourselves to the work of equity, equality, and justice.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8211\'20\'13 Joan E Greve and Maanvi Singh\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.48am BST\par \par \uc2 The Elaine race massacre of 1919 is understood as one of the worst in US history. Parts of the story are still in dispute\par \par \uc2 The history of the race massacre in Elaine, Arkansas, has always been contested.\par \par \uc2 It is widely accepted that in 1919, a group of white men, with the backing of federal troops, tortured and killed scores of Black residents \u8211\'20\'13 the exact number is disputed but assumed to number at least in the hundreds \u8211\'20\'13 who were starting to organize against the exploitation of their labor. The massacre came at the tail end of what would become known as the \u8220\'20\'1Cred summer\u8221\'20\'1D, a season of racial terror fueled by white resentment of the strides Black people were making across the country.\par \par \uc2 But at the time, even these basic contours of what happened in Elaine were stricken from the official record. Local authorities spun a tale of a suppressed sharecropper insurrection designed to seize the land of the area\u8217\'20\'19s white planters. As Ida B Wells, the pioneering journalist and anti-lynching advocate would {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://archive.org/details/TheArkansasRaceRiot/page/n2/mode/2up"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2 , more than 100 Black men and women were indicted in this conspiracy theory. Twelve men were sentenced to death, their convictions ultimately overturned by the supreme court.\par \par \uc2 Other details remain subject to disagreement. The massacre is widely understood to have targeted Black sharecroppers holding a union meeting to discuss how to get fair cotton prices from the white people whose land they worked. But some descendants say this narrative obscures Black landowners in Elaine who were dispossessed through an orchestrated operation to cut down their growing prosperity. Richard Wright echoes this theme in his autobiography Black Boy, when he recounts the 1916 lynching of his uncle in Elaine, targeted because of resentment over his flourishing liquor business. \u8220\'20\'1CIn a way,\u8221\'20\'1D Wright\u8217\'20\'19s daughter Julia tells \uc2 the Guardian\uc2 , \u8220\'20\'1CUncle Hoskins was one of the many canaries in the mineshaft of the Elaine massacre to come.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Read more:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/jun/18/elaine-massacre-red-summer-descendants-history"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18We want our land back\u8217\'20\'19: for descendants of the Elaine massacre, history is far from settled}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.32am BST\par \par \uc2 Bad strategy? How the Republican attack on voting rights could backfire\par \par \uc2 As the coronavirus wreaked havoc around the world, lawmakers in the US were faced with a monumental task: carrying out a presidential election in the middle of a once-in-century pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Concerned about the possibility of virus spread at polling places, Democrats pushed the federal government to approve more funding for states to expand absentee and early-voting options.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was against the idea for a single reason: he thought it would make it harder for Republicans to win. Trump said in a Fox News interview in March of last year that, if early and absentee voting options were expanded as Democrats wanted, \u8220\'20\'1Cyou\u8217\'20\'19d never have a Republican elected in this country again.\u8221\'20\'1D Other Republicans {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gwinnettdailypost.com/local/gwinnett-elections-boards-new-chairwoman-wants-limits-on-no-excuse-absentee-voting-voter-roll-review/article_7df1c274-5715-11eb-a31d-dfa23b30ec62.html/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/elections/supreme-court-gop-attorney-defends-voting-restrictions-saying-they-help-n1259305"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 echoed}}}\uc2  Trump\u8217\'20\'19s argument in recent months, as the party has pushed hundreds of bills to restrict voting access in dozens of states.\par \par \uc2 But voting experts now say the restrictions being approved in Republican-led states may not help the party\u8217\'20\'19s chances in future elections, and in some cases, the laws may even prevent their own supporters from going to the polls. Put simply, in seeking to suppress the vote, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/republicans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Republicans}}}\uc2  may be shooting themselves in the foot.\par \par \uc2 Republican legislators across the country have taken aggressive action to restrict access to the ballot box this year, as Trump has continued to spread the \u8220\'20\'1Cbig lie\u8221\'20\'1D that there was widespread fraud in the presidential election. According to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.brennancenter.org/our-work/research-reports/state-voting-bills-tracker-2021"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Brennan Center for Justice}}}\uc2 , at least 389 bills with restrictive voting provisions have been introduced in 48 states this year, and 22 of those bills have already been enacted.\par \par \uc2 The Republican bills take particular aim at mail-in voting, after \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s supporters used the voting method at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/jun/18/o%09https:/www.pewresearch.org/politics/2020/11/20/the-voting-experience-in-2020/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 disproportionately high rates}}}\uc2  in the 2020 election. However, it is unclear whether restricting mail-in voting will aid Republicans in future elections.\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=3825939"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 recent study}}}\uc2  conducted by a team at the \uc2 Public Policy Institute of California\uc2  found that, while making mail-in voting easier did increase overall turnout, it did not necessarily result in better electoral outcomes for Democrats. In fact, many models indicated that easy access to mail-in voting resulted in slightly better outcomes for Republican candidates.\par \par \uc2 Read more:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/jun/18/republican-voter-restrictions-us-elections"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bad strategy? How the Republican attack on voting rights could backfire}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.31am BST\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s a throwback: Alexander Vindman, the lieutenant colonel and national security aide who served as a key witness in \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s impeachment case, clarified to senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  what that case was all about...\par \par \uc2 enltrI understood it was less about the holdup of military aid and more about the quid pro quo extortion, but what do I know? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/WmG7vdvZyf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/WmG7vdvZyf}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Alexander S. Vindman (@AVindman) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/AVindman/status/1406020540633071621?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 18, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.05am BST\par \par \uc2 The United States Conference of Catholic Bishops\u8217\'20\'19 plan that could end up denying communion to public figures, including \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , will face a major roadblock ahead.\par \par \uc2 The \u8220\'20\'1Cteaching document\u8221\'20\'1D that the bishops are developing, that some hope will rebuke Catholic supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, would ultimately have to be approved by the Vatican, which is unlikely to affirm a pointed or divisive action. The bishops voted 168-55 to develop a report on the meaning of Eucharist \u8211\'20\'13 and the final report, which would be completed sometime this Fall.\par \par \uc2 While conservative members of the church have been pushing for a strong stance against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and those who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, some bishops are hoping the final report will avoid alienating American Catholics, the majority of whom support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Catholics for Choice\uc2 , a group that advocates for reproductive freedom, condemned attempts \u8220\'20\'1Cto use the Eucharist \u8211\'20\'13 the Body of Christ and the central unifying ritual of our church \u8211\'20\'13 as a weapon of punishment\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The group\u8217\'20\'19s president Jamie L. Manson said: \u8220\'20\'1CWe are profoundly saddened that the U.S. Conference of Catholic Bishops has chosen to continue its unholy crusade against President Biden and other pro-choice Catholics, despite the objections of many of their own members, the Vatican, and the overwhelming majority of the people in the pews.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 This post was amended on 21 June 2021 to remove a reference to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  being the first Catholic US president. \uc2 John F Kennedy\uc2  was also a Catholic.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.24pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.50pm BST\par \par \uc2 Across the US, 11 mayors pledged to pay some reparations for slavery to Black residents in their cities \u8211\'20\'13 though details of how much would be paid, and to whom, remain unclear.\par \par \uc2 From the AP:\par \par \uc2 This group of mayors, dubbed Mayors Organized for Reparations and Equity (MORE), is led by Los Angeles Mayor Eric Garcetti and Denver Mayor Michael Hancock. Their stated goal is for these reparations programs to \u8220\'20\'1Cserve as high-profile demonstrations for how the country can more quickly move from conversation to action on reparations for Black Americans,\u8221\'20\'1D according to the group\u8217\'20\'19s website.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLet me be clear: Cities will never have the funds to pay for reparations on our own,\u8221\'20\'1D Garcetti said during a news conference on Friday to announce the group. \u8220\'20\'1CWhen we have the laboratories of cities show that there is much more to embrace than to fear, we know that we can inspire national action as well.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The other mayors are \uc2 Jorge Elorza\uc2  of Providence, Rhode Island; \uc2 Steve Adler\uc2  of Austin, Texas; Steve Schewel of Durham, North Carolina: Esther Manheimer of Asheville, North Carolina: \uc2 Quinton Lucas\uc2  of Kansas City; \uc2 Darrell Steinberg\uc2  of Sacramento, California; Melvin Carter of St. Paul, Minnesota; and Keisha Currin of Tullahassee, Oklahoma.\par \par \uc2 Tullahassee \u8212\'20\'14 a small town of fewer than 200 people in northeast Oklahoma \u8212\'20\'14 is the oldest of the surviving all-Black towns in the states that were founded after the U.S. abolished slavery.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, California launched the first-in-the-nation task force to study and recommend reparations for African Americans:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/jun/01/california-taskforce-reparations-black-americans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 California launches first-in-nation taskforce to study reparations for Black Americans}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.17pm BST\par \par \uc2 Poll: Americans are resuming pre-pandemic activities\par \par \uc2 Many Americans are resuming pre-pandemic activities, even as they worry that restrictions were lifted too quickly, according to a poll from the Associated Press and NORC Center for Public Affairs Research\par \par \uc2 * 34% of those polled said restrictions in their area were lifted too quickly, and 27% said they weren\u8217\'20\'19t lifted quickly enough\par \par \uc2 * Americans were increasingly seeing family, shopping for non-essential items, and going to bars and restaurants in June, compared to May\par \par \uc2 * Only 7% of unvaccinated people said they would definitely get a coronavirus vaccine, and 15% say they probably would.\par \par \uc2 Read more about the poll, based on interviews with 1,125 American adults, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/only-on-ap-lifestyle-coronavirus-pandemic-health-ddfd165dd67e9ec79d4850a1d80734e8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.32pm BST\par \par \uc2 In hunt for Covid\u8217\'20\'19s origin, new studies point away from lab leak theory\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/lauraspinney"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Laura Spinney}}}\uc2  reports:\par \par \uc2 The coronavirus pandemic has raised so many questions as it has continued its inexorable spread across the planet, but perhaps the first of them remains the most contentious: where did Sars-CoV-2 come from?\par \par \uc2 In recent weeks there has been renewed focus on whether it could have escaped from a Chinese laboratory. However, new findings strengthen the case for a natural origin, in what has become a heavily politicised debate.\par \par \uc2 The resurgence of the lab leak theory \u8211\'20\'13 promulgated early last year by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his supporters, before being dismissed \u8211\'20\'13 has been fuelled by the publication of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bbc.com/news/world-us-canada-57352992"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 emails}}}\uc2  in which \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s chief medical adviser, \uc2 Dr Anthony Fauci\uc2 , discussed it, and dissatisfaction with the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s initial efforts to investigate the pandemic\u8217\'20\'19s origins.\par \par \uc2 Most scientists favour the theory that the virus spilled over from an animal to a human in nature. \u8220\'20\'1CThe best scientific evidence points to this,\u8221\'20\'1D said the Wellcome Trust\u8217\'20\'19s director, \uc2 Jeremy Farrar\uc2 , on 4 June.\par \par \uc2 But if there is less evidence to support the lab leak theory, others say, it\u8217\'20\'19s because nobody has searched for it.\par \par \uc2 Read more:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/jun/18/in-hunt-for-covids-origin-new-studies-point-away-from-lab-leak-theory"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 In hunt for Covid\u8217\'20\'19s origin, new studies point away from lab leak theory}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.20pm BST\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s a pattern where the children and relatives of top Biden aides have been securing jobs in the administration, the Washington Post {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/biden-aides-relatives-jobs/2021/06/17/ab504a22-cea4-11eb-8cd2-4e95230cfac2_story.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reports}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 From the Post\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Sean Sullivan\uc2  and Michael Scherer:\par \par \uc2 Barely a week into office, President Biden made a promise that signaled a sharp break from his predecessor: No member of his family would be involved in government.\par \par \uc2 But that vow did not extend to his senior staff and their relatives. In the first few months of Biden\u8217\'20\'19s presidency, at least five children of his top aides have secured coveted jobs in the new administration. They include two sons and a daughter of the White House counselor, the daughter of a deputy White House chief of staff and the daughter of the director of presidential personnel.\par \par \uc2 Beyond children, other relatives of top Biden aides also have secured high-level administration jobs or nominations. They include the wife of White House Chief of Staff \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2  and the sister of White House press secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 President Biden and first lady Jill Biden make their way to board Marine One on the Ellipse of the White House on June 9. (Demetrius Freeman/The Washington Post)\par \par \uc2 Federal law generally prohibits government officials from hiring close relatives or encouraging their hiring, and there is no evidence that any of Biden\u8217\'20\'19s aides have played a role in securing the jobs for their children or other relatives.\par \par \uc2 Read more {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/biden-aides-relatives-jobs/2021/06/17/ab504a22-cea4-11eb-8cd2-4e95230cfac2_story.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.00pm BST\par \par \uc2 Today so far\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s it from me today. My west coast colleague, Maanvi Singh, will take over the blog for the next few hours.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s where the day stands so far:\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  warned that coronavirus \u8220\'20\'1Cremains a serious and deadly threat\u8221\'20\'1D for unvaccinated Americans. In a speech celebrating 300 million vaccine doses being administered since he took office in January, the president pleaded with unvaccinated Americans to get their shots as quickly as possible. \u8220\'20\'1CDeaths and hospitalizations are drastically down in places where people are getting vaccinated,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden said. \u8220\'20\'1CBut unfortunately, cases and hospitalizations are not going down in many places in the lower vaccination rate states. They\u8217\'20\'19re actually going up in some places.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  was heckled as a \u8220\'20\'1Ctraitor\u8221\'20\'1D as he addressed a religious conservative conference in Orlando. The former vice-president received a standing ovation as he took the stage, but a small group of attendees, who were quickly escorted out, shouted insults at Pence as he started speaking. Pence has received criticism from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and some of his most ardent supporters for not attempting to overturn the results of the presidential election.\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  visited Atlanta, Georgia, as part of the Biden administration\u8217\'20\'19s nationwide tour to encourage more Americans to get vaccinated. Visiting a pop-up vaccination site at Ebenezer Baptist Church this afternoon, the vice-president thanked those present for getting their shots. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat you are doing truly is about leadership. These vaccines are safe and effective. It will save your life and lives of people that you love,\u8221\'20\'1D Harris said.\par \par \uc2 * Republicans pushed back against Joe Manchin\u8217\'20\'19s compromise proposal on a voting rights bill, which the Democratic senator laid out in a memo to colleagues this week. Manchin\u8217\'20\'19s bill would include Republican demands like requiring voter ID and allowing controversial purges of voter rolls, but Senate minority leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  said the proposal was still \u8220\'20\'1Can assault on the fundamental idea that states, not the federal government, should decide how to run their own elections\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 * Biden released a proclamation recognizing Juneteenth, which commemorates the end of slavery in America. Biden signed a bill yesterday to make Juneteenth, which falls on June 19, a federal holiday. \u8220\'20\'1CJuneteenth is a day of profound weight and power,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden said in his proclamation. \u8220\'20\'1COn Juneteenth, we recommit ourselves to the work of equity, equality, and justice.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Maanvi will have more coming up, so stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.45pm BST\par \par \uc2 Democratic congressman \uc2 Ted Lieu\uc2  accused the US Conference of Catholic Bishops of hypocrisy for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2021/jun/18/republicans-voting-rights-democrats-manchin-us-politics-live?page=with:block-60ccef418f08f35298756262#block-60ccef418f08f35298756262"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 taking steps}}}\uc2  toward rebuking Catholic politicians who receive Communion and support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDear @USCCB: I\u8217\'20\'19m Catholic and you are hypocrites,\u8221\'20\'1D Lieu said on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Referring to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s attorney general, Lieu added, \u8220\'20\'1CYou did not tell Bill Barr, a Catholic, not to take communion when he expanded killing human beings with the death penalty. You are being nakedly partisan and you should be ashamed. Another reason you are losing membership.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 enltrDear {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/USCCB?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @USCCB}}}\uc2  : I\u8217\'20\'19m Catholic and you are hypocrites. You did not tell Bill Barr, a Catholic, not to take communion when he expanded killing human beings with the death penalty. You are being nakedly partisan and you should be ashamed. Another reason you are losing membership. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/kpIYRolnHD"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/kpIYRolnHD}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Ted Lieu\uc2  (@tedlieu) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/tedlieu/status/1405967402894958596?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 18, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 Lieu also joined dozens of other Catholic Democrats in Congress in signing on to a \u8220\'20\'1Cstatement of principles\u8221\'20\'1D urging the USCCB not to continue with their efforts.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe believe the separation of church and state allows for our faith to inform our public duties and best serve our constituents,\u8221\'20\'1D the statement says.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Sacrament of Holy Communion is central to the life of practicing Catholics, and the weaponization of the Eucharist to Democratic lawmakers for their support of a woman\u8217\'20\'19s safe and legal access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is contradictory.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.31pm BST\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has arrived in his home state of Delaware, where he will be spending the weekend. The president did not take any questions from reporters as he boarded and later exited Marine One.\par \par \uc2 enltrPresident Biden arrives in New Castle, Delaware {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/Dhx3wsqVWM"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/Dhx3wsqVWM}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 The Hill (@thehill) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/thehill/status/1405984973224546307?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 18, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.14pm BST\par \par \uc2 Jill Biden will travel to Jackson, Mississippi, and Nashville, Tennessee, on Tuesday to visit coronavirus vaccination sites and encourage more Americans to get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 The first lady, the vice-president and the second gentleman have recently been participating in a nationwide tour to areas with lower vaccination rates to encourage people to get their shots.\par \par \uc2 enltr?? HAPPENING TUESDAY: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/FLOTUS?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @FLOTUS}}}\uc2  ?? heads to Jackson, Mississippi and Nashville, Tennessee {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/rsheXMmMLf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/rsheXMmMLf}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Michael LaRosa (@MichaelLaRosa46) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/MichaelLaRosa46/status/1405980446052302849?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 18, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 Speaking at the historically Black Clark Atlanta University in Georgia today, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  urged Americans to reject misinformation about the vaccines and protect themselves against the virus.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLet\u8217\'20\'19s recognize that we have power in every moment of crisis, including this one,\u8221\'20\'1D Harris said.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.56pm BST\par \par \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , the former US vice-president, has been heckled as a \u8220\'20\'1Ctraitor\u8221\'20\'1D for his refusal to overturn last year\u8217\'20\'19s election result during a speech to a gathering of religious conservatives.\par \par \uc2 Pence, who is widely seen as laying the groundwork for a White House run in 2024, had entered an auditorium in Orlando, Florida to a standing ovation on Friday. But a small group began shouted abuse including \u8220\'20\'1Ctraitor!\u8221\'20\'1D as he began a 28-minute speech. The dissenters were quickly escorted out by police.\par \par \uc2 Earlier, in a corridor outside the ballroom, an attendee named Rick Hurley, wearing a red \u8220\'20\'1CMake America great again\u8221\'20\'1D cap, also vented his frustration over Pence\u8217\'20\'19s role in certifying \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s defeat on 6 January amid false claims of voter fraud.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe need to start fighting!\u8221\'20\'1D Hurley shouted at anyone who would listen. \u8220\'20\'1CWe need to stop being so damned nice. What the hell\u8217\'20\'19s going on? Why is Pence coming today? \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has his pen in his back still.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Before being taken aside by police, he also remarked: \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m ready to fight. I\u8217\'20\'19m going to boo him off stage. I\u8217\'20\'19ll take the bullet. I\u8217\'20\'19ll walk to the front of the stage and look him in the eye and and say, \u8216\'20\'18What are you doing here?\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 In an interview, Hurley said he had been at the US Capitol on 6 January. \u8220\'20\'1CI want to know why Pence is here today.\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CHe stabbed \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the back and took the coins like Judas.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/jun/18/mike-pence-conservative-christian-conference-heckled"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Conservative Christians jeer \u8216\'20\'18traitor\u8217\'20\'19 Pence for refusing to overturn election}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.36pm BST\par \par \uc2 Dozens of Catholic Democrats in Congress signed on to a letter denouncing the efforts to rebuke lawmakers who receive Communion and support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe believe the separation of church and state allows for our faith to inform our public duties and best serve our constituents,\u8221\'20\'1D the letter says.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Sacrament of Holy Communion is central to the life of practicing Catholics, and the weaponization of the Eucharist to Democratic lawmakers for their support of a woman\u8217\'20\'19s safe and legal access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is contradictory.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 enltrAs Catholic Democrats, we are committed to making real the basic principles that are at the heart of Catholic social teaching: helping the oppressed, protecting the least among us & ensuring that all are given opportunities to share in the blessings of this great country. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/nZHeUVfP2j"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/nZHeUVfP2j}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Rosa DeLauro (@rosadelauro) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/rosadelauro/status/1405950124753367040?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 18, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 The lawmakers who signed the letter -- including Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez, J oaquin Castro and \uc2 Ted Lieu\uc2  -- also made a point to list some of the Republican policies that they say contradict Catholic teachings.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNo elected officials have been threatened with being denied the Eucharist as they support and have supported policies contrary to the Church teachings, including supporting the death penalty, separating migrant children from their parents, denying asylum to those seeking safety in the United States, limiting assistance for the hungry and food insecure, and denying rights and dignity to immigrants,\u8221\'20\'1D the letter says.\par \par \uc2 The lawmakers encouraged the US Conference of Catholic Bishops to not continue with its efforts, saying, \u8220\'20\'1CWe solemnly urge you to not move forward and deny this most holy of all sacraments, the source and the summit of the whole work of the gospel over one issue.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.18pm BST\par \par \uc2 'I don\u8217\'20\'19t think that\u8217\'20\'19s going to happen,' Biden says of efforts to block him from receiving Communion\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  took a couple questions from reporters after concluding his prepared remarks on his administration\u8217\'20\'19s coronavirus vaccination efforts.\par \par \uc2 One reporter asked the president for his response to the US Conference of Catholic Bishops taking steps toward rebuking Catholic politicians, such as Biden, who receive Communion and support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s a private matter, and I don\u8217\'20\'19t think that\u8217\'20\'19s going to happen,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden replied.\par \par \uc2 The AP {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/joe-biden-government-and-politics-religion-ea0179cd6c8eddbf66693a7e2b28e2da"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has more details}}}\uc2  on the USCCB\u8217\'20\'19s decision:\par \par \uc2 U.S. Catholic bishops overwhelmingly approved the drafting of a \u8216\'20\'18teaching document\u8217\'20\'19 that many of them hope will rebuke Catholic politicians, including [Biden], for receiving Communion despite their support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 The result of the vote \u8212\'20\'14 168 in favor and 55 against \u8212\'20\'14 was announced Friday near the end of a three-day meeting of the U.S. Conference of Catholic Bishops that was held virtually.\par \par \uc2 The bishops had cast their votes privately on Thursday after nearly three hours of impassioned debate.\par \par \uc2 Supporters of the measure said a strong rebuke of Biden was needed because of his recent actions protecting and expanding \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, while opponents warned that such action would portray the bishops as a partisan force during a time of bitter political divisions across the country.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.06pm BST\par \par \uc2 Covid 'remains a serious and deadly threat' for unvaccinated people, Biden says\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has just finished delivering remarks on reaching 300 million coronavirus vaccine doses administered since he took office in January.\par \par \uc2 The president praised the \u8220\'20\'1Cwhole of government response\u8221\'20\'1D that has allowed millions of Americans to get vaccinated. As of today, 65% of American adults have received at least one vaccine shot.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAbove all, we got here because of the American people,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden said.\par \par \uc2 enltrPres. Biden: "We've built equity into the heart of our vaccination program from day one." {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/xY8G8YiI04"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/xY8G8YiI04}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/4HBMqXSsfN"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/4HBMqXSsfN}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 ABC News (@ABC) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ABC/status/1405963038558240768?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 18, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 The president also touted his administration\u8217\'20\'19s efforts to get Americans of color vaccinated, while acknowledging more works needs to be done in that regard.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve built equity into the heart of our vaccination program from day one,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe more we close the racial gap in vaccination rates, the more lives we\u8217\'20\'19ll save.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 While looking ahead to the country\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Csummer of joy\u8221\'20\'1D as pandemic-related restrictions are relaxed, Biden emphasized that coronavirus \u8220\'20\'1Cremains a serious and deadly threat\u8221\'20\'1D for unvaccinated Americans.\par \par \uc2 The president also warned that the delta variant of the virus could pose an even worse threat to unvaccinated people. Speaking directly to unvaccinated Americans, Biden said, \u8220\'20\'1CAct. Act now.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.07pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.53pm BST\par \par \uc2 As \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  prepares to deliver an update on US vaccination efforts, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is in Atlanta, Georgia, to encourage more Americans to get their shots.\par \par \uc2 Speaking at the historically Black Clark Atlanta University this afternoon, Harris denounced misinformation about the coronavirus vaccines, saying, \u8220\'20\'1CLet\u8217\'20\'19s arm ourselves with the truth.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 enltrHarris speaks about the dramatic drops in COVID infections and deaths over the past five months: "Let's recognize that we have power in every moment of crisis, including this one...When the American people come together, in the spirit of community, we can do anything. Anything." {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/SIJlFEh57Z"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/SIJlFEh57Z}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 CBS News (@CBSNews) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/CBSNews/status/1405946191590875138?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 18, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 The vice-president also noted that most of the Americans currently hospitalized with coronavirus are unvaccinated.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLet\u8217\'20\'19s recognize that we have power in every moment of crisis, including this one,\u8221\'20\'1D Harris said. \u8220\'20\'1CWhen the American people come together, in the spirit of community, we can do anything. Anything.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.33pm BST\par \par \uc2 As we prepare for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s remarks on the ongoing coronavirus vaccination efforts in the US, some reporters shared photos of a rainbow-lit hallway at the White House.\par \par \uc2 The colorful lights appear to be an homage to LGBTQ+ Pride month, which began on June 1.\par \par \uc2 enltrWhite House hallway lit up in rainbow colors for Pride Month. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/lNFysOB31z"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/lNFysOB31z}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 darlene superville (@dsupervilleap) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/dsupervilleap/status/1405949935623847943?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 18, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.20pm BST\par \par \uc2 300 million vaccine shots administered since Biden took office, White House announces\par \par \uc2 More than 300 coronavirus vaccine doses have been administered since \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  took office in January, the White House announced in a press release.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CToday, thanks to the President\u8217\'20\'19s COVID-19 strategy, the virus is in retreat. 300 million shots have been administered in 150 days, COVID-19 cases and deaths have decreased by more than 90 percent, and the economy is experiencing the strongest rebound in decades,\u8221\'20\'1D the White House said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe results are clear: America is starting to look like America again, and entering a summer of joy and freedom.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A man receives a shot at the FEMA-supported COVID-19 vaccination site at Valencia State College in Orlando. Photograph: Paul Hennessy/SOPA Images/REX/\uc2 Shutterstock\par \par \uc2 The White House noted that 65% of American adults have now received at least one vaccine dose, and 55% of adults in the US are fully vaccinated. Additionally, 15 states and DC have gotten more than 70% of adults at least partially vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 However, it is still unclear whether the US will hit Biden\u8217\'20\'19s goal of having 70% of all American adults at least partially vaccinated by July 4, when America celebrates its Independence Day.\par \par \uc2 Biden is expected to deliver an update on his administration\u8217\'20\'19s vaccination efforts at any moment, so stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.10pm BST\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has endorsed a Republican Senate candidate running against Lisa Murkowski, the Republican senator from Alaska who is up for reelection next year.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLisa Murkowski is bad for Alaska,\u8221\'20\'1D Trump said in a new statement. \u8220\'20\'1CHer vote to confirm Biden \u8217\'20\'19s Interior Secretary was a vote to kill long sought for, and approved, ANWR, and Alaska jobs. Murkowski has got to go! Kelly Tshibaka is the candidate who can beat Murkowski\u8212\'20\'14and she will.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The endorsement is not a surprise, given that the former president has frequently criticized Murkowski since she voted to convict Trump in his second impeachment trial earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 Trump said of Tshibaka, \u8220\'20\'1CKelly is a fighter who stands for Alaska values and America First.... Kelly is a powerful supporter of the Second Amendment and JOBS! I look forward to campaigning in Alaska for Kelly Tshibaka. She has my Complete and Total Endorsement!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2021/04/27/politics/kfile-kelly-tshibaka-ex-gay-organization-witchcraft/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 According to CNN}}}\uc2 , Tshibaka has previously written in support of an \u8220\'20\'1Cex-gay\u8221\'20\'1D Christian organization that promoted {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/apr/07/i-felt-this-crushing-guilt-how-faith-based-lgbtq-conversion-practices-harm-young-australians"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 conversion therapy}}}\uc2  for LGBTQ+ individuals.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.38pm BST\par \par \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  heckled at conservative Christian conference\par \par \uc2 Former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  was heckled during his speech at a conservative Christian conference on Friday, with some attendees shouting \u8220\'20\'1Ctraitor!\u8221\'20\'1D according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.forbes.com/sites/andrewsolender/2021/06/18/traitor-pence-faces-hecklers-during-conservative-christian-summit/?sh=2afed8d27d98"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Forbes}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is great to be back with so many patriots, dedicated to faith and freedom and the road to the majority,\u8221\'20\'1D Pence remarked.\par \par \uc2 Pence, speaking at the Trump supporter-filled Faith & Freedom symposium, was cheered when he took the stage, NBC News journalist Ali Vitali {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/alivitali/status/1405927230254030858"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2 . Less than thirty seconds into his address, however, yelling emerged from the audience.\par \par \uc2 enltrPence gets drowned out by hecklers at the Faith & Freedom Coalition summit, some of whom appear to be chanting "traitor!" {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/pAQxavsK3O"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/pAQxavsK3O}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Andrew Solender (@AndrewSolender) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/AndrewSolender/status/1405928997880533003?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 18, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 One woman who yelled \u8220\'20\'1Ctraitor!\u8221\'20\'1D was escorted out, Vitali reported.\par \par \uc2 Although Pence served Trump dutifully, some of the ex-president\u8217\'20\'19s supporters turned on him. On 6 January, a mob of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s supporters breached the US Capitol in an attempt to overturn the election.\par \par \uc2 Some insurrectionists shouted {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/jan/10/hang-mike-pence-twitter-stops-phrase-trending-capitol-breach"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Chang Mike Pence}}}\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D shortly after Trump remarked that he would feel \u8220\'20\'1Cvery disappointed\u8221\'20\'1D if his then-deputy didn\u8217\'20\'19t overturn the results, per {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.forbes.com/sites/andrewsolender/2021/01/06/trump-vows-to-never-concede-unseat-republicans-who-break-with-him/?sh=80de92b1493e"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Forbes}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.43pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.00pm BST\par \par \uc2 Today so far\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s where the day stands so far:\par \par \uc2 * Republicans pushed back against Joe Manchin\u8217\'20\'19s compromise proposal on a voting rights bill, which the Democratic senator laid out in a memo to colleagues this week. Manchin\u8217\'20\'19s bill would include Republican demands like requiring voter ID and allowing voter purges, but Senate minority leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  said the proposal was still \u8220\'20\'1Can assault on the fundamental idea that states, not the federal government, should decide how to run their own elections\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  released a proclamation recognizing Juneteenth, which commemorates the end of slavery in America. Biden signed a bill yesterday to make Juneteenth, which falls on June 19, a federal holiday. \u8220\'20\'1CJuneteenth is a day of profound weight and power,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden said in his proclamation. \u8220\'20\'1COn Juneteenth, we recommit ourselves to the work of equity, equality, and justice.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is visiting Atlanta, Georgia, as part of her tour to encourage more Americans to get vaccinated against coronavirus. Visiting a pop-up vaccination site at Ebenezer Baptist Church this afternoon, the vice-president thanked those present for getting their shots. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat you are doing truly is about leadership. These vaccines are safe and effective. It will save your life and lives of people that you love,\u8221\'20\'1D Harris said.\par \par \uc2 The blog will have more coming up, so stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.49pm BST\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is nominating former congresswoman \uc2 Xochitl Torres\uc2  Small to a senior role in the department of agriculture, the White House just announced in a statement.\par \par \uc2 If confirmed by the Senate, Torres Small, who lost her reelection bid last year, would become undersecretary of rural development at the USDA.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn the midst of the COVID-19 crisis, Representative Torres Small kept a rural hospital from closing its doors, improved constituent access to healthcare over the phone, and helped secure tens of millions of dollars for broadband in New Mexico through \uc2 USDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s ReConnect Program,\u8221\'20\'1D the White House said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThroughout her career, Torres Small has employed her experience organizing in vulnerable, rural communities to achieve lasting investments that combat persistent poverty.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Torres Small served only one term in the House before being defeated in November by Republican \uc2 Yvette Herrell\uc2 , who won the race in New Mexico\u8217\'20\'19s 2nd Congressional District by about 7 points.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.29pm BST\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  toured the pop-up vaccination site at Ebenezer Baptist Church in Atlanta, and she thanked those who are getting their shots today.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat you are doing truly is about leadership. These vaccines are safe and effective. It will save your life and lives of people that you love,\u8221\'20\'1D the vice-president said, per a press pool report. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re here to say thank you.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Harris toured the vaccination site with several Democratic lawmakers, including Georgia senators \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and \uc2 Jon Ossoff\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 After Harris made her remarks to the newly vaccinated people at the site, Warnock joked, \u8220\'20\'1CThe vice-president preached a good sermon today. Amen!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.10pm BST\par \par \uc2 Harris visits Georgia to encourage coronavirus vaccinations\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has arrived in Atlanta, Georgia, where she will continue her tour of southern states to encourage more Americans to get their coronavirus vaccines.\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  exits Air Force Two, Friday, June 18, 2021, on arrival to Atlanta. Photograph: Jacquelyn Martin/AP\par \par \uc2 Harris is scheduled to tour a pop-up vaccination site at Ebenezer Baptist Church, where \uc2 Martin Luther King Jr\uc2  once preached, and then deliver remarks at a vaccination mobilization event at \uc2 Clark Atlanta University\uc2 , a historically Black college.\par \par \uc2 The vice-president will also later participate in a conversation on voting rights with community leaders at the university.\par \par \uc2 As part of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cmonth of action\u8221\'20\'1D to increase vaccination rates, Harris kicked off her southern tour with a trip to South Carolina earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CVaccination gives protection,\u8221\'20\'1D Harris said in Greenville. \u8220\'20\'1CThis act, in a way, is a projection of love thy neighbor.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.50pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.47pm BST\par \par \uc2 The White House chief of staff issued a warning about the potential for coronavirus case numbers to rise in parts of the country where vaccination rates are low.\par \par \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2  responded to a tweet noting that the US seven-day average of new cases is now at the lowest point since the pandemic started last year.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe progress is great to see. But let\u8217\'20\'19s be clear: parts of the country where vaccination rates are low will see cases climb again,\u8221\'20\'1D Klain said on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 enltrThe progress is great to see. But let's be clear: parts of the country where vaccination rates are low will see cases climb again. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/YPD8Q1xvnk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/YPD8Q1xvnk}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Ronald Klain\uc2  (@WHCOS) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/WHCOS/status/1405889471166431236?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 18, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 The chief of staff\u8217\'20\'19s warning comes as the Delta variant of coronavirus, first identified in India, is becoming the globally dominant variant of the virus.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Delta variant is well on its way to becoming the dominant variant globally because of its increased transmissibility,\u8221\'20\'1D Soumya Swaminathan, the chief scientist of the World Health Organization, said today.\par \par \uc2 Follow \uc2 the Guardian\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s coronavirus live blog for the latest on the pandemic:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2021/jun/18/coronavirus-live-news-us-launches-32bn-virus-treatment-program-england-opens-vaccines-to-all-adults"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Covid live: Delta variant becoming globally dominant, WHO says as expert calls Africa\u8217\'20\'19s case trajectory \u8216\'20\'18very, very concerning\u8217\'20\'19}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.27pm BST\par \par \uc2 On 7 November 2020, after several days of vote-counting, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lost the US presidential election. More than 60 unsuccessful lawsuits and one insurrection later, Trump has still lost the election, but the former president refuses to accept defeat.\par \par \uc2 Egged on by a group of sycophants and fantasists, including a small-time Pennsylvania politician, a host on a far-right news network, and the CEO of a pillow company, Trump now plans to hold rallies at the end of June where he is likely to continue his fraudulent claims of a stolen election.\par \par \uc2 Despite the election having been repeatedly investigated and declared \u8220\'20\'1Cthe most secure in American history\u8221\'20\'1D by a group of experts, the former president is said to be convinced the election result will be overturned.\par \par \uc2 As are those in his close circle fighting a series of quixotic battles on his behalf. Mike Lindell, the CEO of MyPillow and a Trump confidant who claims to have evidence that shows voting machines were hacked by China, told \uc2 the Guardian\uc2  Trump would be returned to office by August or \u8211\'20\'13 at the latest \u8211\'20\'13 September.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWith me they just keep saying: \u8216\'20\'18It\u8217\'20\'19s a conspiracy, Mike Lindell \u8211\'20\'13 he\u8217\'20\'19s crazy, blah blah blah,\u8217\'20\'19 all this stuff,\u8221\'20\'1D Lindell said. \u8220\'20\'1CBut I think it gives the whole country hope because they know me and they know I wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be out there if I wasn\u8217\'20\'19t 100%.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/jun/18/trump-election-lie-mike-lindell-mypillow"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The sycophantic inner circle egging on Trump \u8211\'20\'13 and fueling his big lie}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.27pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.07pm BST\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is facing some barriers in passing his rather ambitious agenda, namely senators Joe Manchin of West Virginia and Kyrsten Sinema of Arizona. Both are refusing to toe the party line on abolishing the filibuster and on issues such as voting rights. So, which side is most likely to win out?\par \par \uc2 In the latest episode of \uc2 the Guardian\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Politics Weekly Extra podcast, Jonathan Freedland and Prof Sarah Binder discuss why two Democratic senators are proving a thorn in Biden\u8217\'20\'19s side:\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.07pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.52pm BST\par \par \uc2 Ian Millhiser of Vox explains that Joe Manchin \u8217\'20\'19s compromise proposal on a voting rights bill includes some provisions that will likely irritate his fellow Democrats.\par \par \uc2 However, if it could become law, and that is big if with the Senate filibuster in place, it would mark a significant achievement for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Millhiser {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vox.com/22537146/joe-manchin-voting-rights-for-the-people-john-lewis-act-gerrymandering-voter-id-democrats"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 writes}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 Manchin\u8217\'20\'19s list includes many reforms drawn from the For the People Act as well as from a companion voting rights bill known as the \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  Voting Rights Act. Significantly, Manchin endorsed banning partisan gerrymandering \u8212\'20\'14 a high priority for both small-d democrats and large-D Democrats, who want to prevent the GOP from seizing control of the House of Representatives with rigged congressional maps.\par \par \uc2 Not everything on Manchin\u8217\'20\'19s list will delight his fellow Democrats. He proposes a nationwide voter ID law, for example, although not an especially strict one. And he wants states to be able to engage in \u8216\'20\'18maintenance of voter rolls\u8217\'20\'19\u8212\'20\'14 purging names from the state\u8217\'20\'19s list of registered voters \u8212\'20\'14 using state and federal documents to identify which voters should be purged....\par \par \uc2 The West Virginia senator\u8217\'20\'19s proposal, in other words, falls short of the dreams of Democratic leaders and voting rights advocates who rallied behind the For the People Act, which passed the House in March....\par \par \uc2 But Democrats and other supporters of democracy would have a great deal to celebrate if Manchin\u8217\'20\'19s list of proposed reforms did become law. And his decision to release such a comprehensive and well-thought-out list of proposals should give some hope to reformers, as it suggests Manchin does want to pass a voting rights bill \u8212\'20\'14 even if it isn\u8217\'20\'19t the specific bill that many advocates want.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.33pm BST\par \par \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  is also participating in a virtual town hall with his former attorney general, \uc2 Eric Holder\uc2 , on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The conversation will be focused on \u8220\'20\'1Cthe importance of getting involved in the redistricting process to strengthen our democracy and support Senate action on voting rights legislation\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 enltrOn Monday, I\u8217\'20\'19m joining {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/EricHolder?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @EricHolder}}}\uc2  for a grassroots conversation with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/allontheline?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @AllOnTheLine}}}\uc2  to talk about why redistricting matters and how the Senate can help protect our democracy. Join us: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/gXAnHHIVlL"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/gXAnHHIVlL}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  (@BarackObama) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BarackObama/status/1405874188406210560?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 18, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 The town hall is hosted by All on the Line, an initiative \uc2 Obama{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/politics/2019/08/26/barack-obama-launches-initiative-take-partisan-gerrymandering/2127235001/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 launched}}}\uc2  in 2019 to train local activists on how to get involved in the redistricting process.\par \par \uc2 Since leaving the justice department, Holder has also become actively involved in the redistricting fight, serving as the chairman of the National Democratic Redistricting Committee.\par \par \uc2 The town hall comes as Democrats \u8217\'20\'19 voting rights bills remain stalled in the Senate because of widespread Republican opposition.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.09pm BST\par \par \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  spoke about the significance of Juneteenth as part of an ABC News special, which is scheduled to air tonight.\par \par \uc2 Good Morning America\u8217\'20\'19s Michael Strahan asked \uc2 Obama\uc2  how Americans can regain some of the hope that his candidacy and presidency sparked for so many.\par \par \uc2 enltr"A lot of people feel like they've lost hope," {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/michaelstrahan?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @michaelstrahan}}}\uc2  says to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BarackObama?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @BarackObama}}}\uc2 . "How can people get that hope back?"\par \par \uc2 Hear former Pres. Obama's answer\u8212\'20\'14and tune into "Juneteenth: Together We Triumph," TONIGHT at 9|8c and tomorrow on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hulu?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @Hulu}}}\uc2 . {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/SoulofaNation?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #SoulofaNation}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/N8HEACRATr"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/N8HEACRATr}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 ABC News (@ABC) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ABC/status/1405875552695820290?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 18, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou get hope back from, for me at least, taking the long view and recognizing that resilience, determination, the ability to deal with setbacks and disappointments and keep going, that those are qualities that can carry us forward,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Obama\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAnd no one has exhibited that more historically in this country than African Americans.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.52pm BST\par \par \uc2 The Hill has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thehill.com/homenews/state-watch/559021-40-year-march-only-one-state-doesnt-recognize-juneteenth?userid=204873"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an excellent story}}}\uc2  about the 40-year fight to make Juneteenth a federal holiday:\par \par \uc2 The march from unofficial holiday to a formal day off for most federal employees started in Texas, more than a century after Union Gen. Gordon Granger issued an 1865 order freeing the remaining 250,000 or so Black people who were still enslaved in the state, two years after the Emancipation Proclamation.\par \par \uc2 It took until 1979 for Texas to formally recognize the holiday, after legislators approved a measure introduced by state Rep. Al Edwards (D), a veteran civil rights activist who marched with Martin Luther King Jr. Edwards introduced the bill in the first of his thirteen terms in office....\par \par \uc2 Every president since former President Clinton\u8217\'20\'19s administration has taken the opportunity to issue statements or remarks honoring the holiday. And for the last dozen years, \uc2 Rep. Sheila Jackson Lee\uc2  (D-Texas), who represents the same Houston area that Edwards represented in the Texas legislature, has introduced a measure in Congress to make the day the nation\u8217\'20\'19s [12th] official holiday.\par \par \uc2 Other states slowly followed Texas\u8217\'20\'19s lead: Florida adopted a Juneteenth holiday in 1991, Oklahoma in 1994 and Minnesota in 1996. Thirty-one states adopted the holiday between 2000 and 2009, and another 13 did so in the decade that followed.\par \par \uc2 Now that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has signed the bill to make Juneteenth a federal holiday, all states are expected to recognize the day that commemorates the end of slavery in America.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.32pm BST\par \par \uc2 Biden releases proclamation recognizing Juneteenth, 'a day of profound weight and power'\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has released a proclamation in recognition of Juneteenth, which commemorates the end of slavery in America.\par \par \uc2 The president signed a law yesterday officially making Juneteenth a federal holiday, after the House and the Senate passed the legislation earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 enltrJuneteenth is officially a federal holiday.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 President Biden (@POTUS) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/POTUS/status/1405621303256698880?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 17, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn its celebration of freedom, Juneteenth is a day that should be recognized by all Americans. And that is why I am proud to have consecrated Juneteenth as our newest national holiday,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden said in his proclamation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CJuneteenth is a day of profound weight and power. A day in which we remember the moral stain and terrible toll of slavery on our country \u8211\'20\'13- what I\u8217\'20\'19ve long called America\u8217\'20\'19s original sin. A long legacy of systemic racism, inequality, and inhumanity.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBut it is a day that also reminds us of our incredible capacity to heal, hope, and emerge from our darkest moments with purpose and resolve.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Looking ahead to future anti-racism efforts, Biden added, \u8220\'20\'1COn Juneteenth, we recommit ourselves to the work of equity, equality, and justice.... There is still more work to do.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Juneteenth is celebrated on June 19, but many offices are observing it today because the 19th falls on a Saturday this year. Federal workers have today off as well.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/jun/17/juneteenth-slavery-federal-holiday-biden-signs-bill"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Juneteenth becomes federal holiday celebrating end of slavery in US}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.25pm BST\par \par \uc2 Christopher Staudinger reports for the Louisiana Illuminator and Floodlight, a nonprofit that partners with \uc2 the Guardian\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 One morning in September, word of layoffs began to spread quickly through \uc2 Marathon Petroleum\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s refinery in the small industrial community of Garyville, Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 Seven months into the pandemic, workers at the oil refining plant thought they would be spared the fate of their colleagues at other facilities, who had already been jettisoned into a daunting job market.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThrough the morning, we were seeing people get the phone call and not come back,\u8221\'20\'1D said one maintenance engineer, who lost his job after nearly a decade at the facility. \u8220\'20\'1CEverybody was on pins and needles waiting for the call.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Last year, Marathon laid off 1,920 workers across the US despite taking $2.1bn in federal tax benefits meant to cushion the pandemic\u8217\'20\'19s blow to the economy, according to a report from BailoutWatch.\par \par \uc2 The worker interviewed for this story, who asked to remain anonymous for fear of difficulty finding a job, is still unemployed. He and his wife had plans to start a family, which are now on hold. And he is competing with more than 18,000 oil, gas and manufacturing workers in Louisiana who lost jobs last year.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m a born and raised Louisianan. So I\u8217\'20\'19m very much trying to stay in the area,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/jun/18/marathon-petroleum-job-cuts-pandemic-benefits"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 A US oil company cut nearly 2,000 jobs \u8211\'20\'13 and reaped $2.1bn in pandemic benefits}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.34pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.25pm BST\par \par \uc2 Significant hurdles remain for Manchin compromise on voting rights bill\par \par \uc2 Greetings from Washington, live blog readers.\par \par \uc2 Most of the city\u8217\'20\'19s attention was focused on the supreme court yesterday, as the justices dismissed a Republican challenge to the Affordable Care Act. However, there were also some significant developments on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 Senate minority leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  signaled he would not support the compromise proposed by Democratic senator Joe Manchin on voting rights. Manchin\u8217\'20\'19s proposal incorporates some Republican ideas to help attract bipartisan support, and voting rights leaders like \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  have signaled they can get behind the bill.\par \par \uc2 But it\u8217\'20\'19s seeming very unlikely that any Republicans will back Manchin\u8217\'20\'19s bill after McConnell released a statement denouncing the proposal yesterday.\par \par \uc2 enltrDemocrats can change the packaging, but their plan for a power-grab of America\u8217\'20\'19s electoral system is still rotten to the core. My full statement: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/HLRuDSZ3lS"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/HLRuDSZ3lS}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Leader McConnell (@LeaderMcConnell) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/LeaderMcConnell/status/1405611368615718922?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 17, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSenate Democrats seem to have reached a so-called \u8216\'20\'18compromise\u8217\'20\'19 election takeover among themselves. In reality, the plan endorsed by \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  is no compromise,\u8221\'20\'1D McConnell said.\par \par \uc2 Comparing the compromise to the For the People Act, Democrats \u8217\'20\'19 original election reform bill, McConnell said, \u8220\'20\'1CAnd it still retains S. 1\u8217\'20\'19s rotten core: an assault on the fundamental idea that states, not the federal government, should decide how to run their own elections.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It seems possible for Manchin\u8217\'20\'19s proposal to attract the support of all 50 Democratic senators, but unless he can get 10 Republicans to join them, McConnell will be able to successfully filibuster the legislation.\par \par \uc2 So the question becomes: is Manchin willing to alter or end the Senate filibuster for voting rights? His recent comments indicate he probably wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t, but time will tell.\par \par \uc2 The blog will have more coming up, so stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210618eh6i00264\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
