\par\par \uc2 Film\par \b \uc2 How Hollywood finally reckoned with reproductive rights in America\b0\par\par\uc2 Adrian Horton \par \uc2 1,815 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 June 2021\par 20:12 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 As states across the US see access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restricted, three eerily similar films about teenage girls caught in an unfair system shine a necessary light\par \par \uc2 The Hulu comedy Plan B, released last week, portrays a hectic weekend in the life of two South Dakota teenage girls, with the zaniness of Booksmart and Superbad-esque raunch updated for 2021. But whereas the Booksmart girls sought to party, and the teens in Superbad tried to get laid, inseparable Sunny (Kuhoo Verma) and Lupe (Victoria Moroles) have a more practical, urgent mission: obtain morning-after contraception at the nearest Planned Parenthood, three hours away after a pharmacist invokes the state\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/state-policy/explore/refusing-provide-health-services"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cconscience clause\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  to deny the \u8220\'20\'1CPlan B\u8221\'20\'1D pill to 17-year-old Sunny \u8211\'20\'13 an obscure barrier to reproductive healthcare writers / high school friends Joshua Levy and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://browngirlmagazine.com/2021/05/plan-b-co-writer-prathi-srinivasan-interview/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Prathiksha Srinivasan}}}\uc2  pulled directly from real life.\par \par \uc2 The film\u8217\'20\'19s structure \u8211\'20\'13 two yin-yang best friends careening through the obstacle course that is America\u8217\'20\'19s reproductive healthcare system, potholed with extra hijinks played for laughs \u8211\'20\'13 is remarkably similar to \uc2 HBO\uc2  Max\u8217\'20\'19s 2020 film {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2020/sep/10/unpregnant-abortion-road-trip-hbo-max"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Unpregnant}}}\uc2 , co-written and directed by Rachel Lee Goldenberg and based on the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.harpercollins.com/products/unpregnant-jenni-hendriksted-caplan?variant=32117329461282"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2019 YA book}}}\uc2  by Ted Caplan and Jenni Hendriks. That film follows two high schoolers who hit the road to find the closest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider that doesn\u8217\'20\'19t require parental consent for a minor (a 14-hour drive from their home town of Columbia, Missouri, to Albuquerque, New Mexico). Veronica (Haley Lu Richardson) and Bailey\u8217\'20\'19s (Barbie Ferreira) hilarious, fundamentally unnecessary journey mirrors that of pregnant Autumn (Sidney Flanigan) and her best friend/cousin Skylar (Talia Ryder) in Eliza Hittman\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2020/mar/09/never-rarely-sometimes-always-review-powerful-abortion-drama"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Never Rarely Sometimes Always}}}\uc2 , released in theaters last year and now streaming on \uc2 HBO\uc2  Max. A clenched, somber drama, Never Rarely Sometimes Always is the tonal opposite of Unpregnant and Plan B, but the scope and stakes are essentially the same: Autumn and Skylar journey to a Planned Parenthood in New York City from their rural Pennsylvania town, where the only reproductive healthcare available is a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2019/aug/16/georgia-abortion-crisis-pregnancy-centers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 crisis pregnancy center}}}\uc2  that deceptively counsels against \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -mindedness.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Call it the reproductive rights road-trip mini-genre: against the backdrop on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.aclu.org/issues/reproductive-freedom/abortion/last-clinics-standing"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 eclipsing \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  access}}}\uc2  in the US \u8211\'20\'13 a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2019/jun/18/us-anti-abortion-bans-backlash-gerrymandering"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wave of extreme, unconstitutional \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  bans }}}\uc2  in 2019, state-level restrictions which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2016/feb/24/us-abortion-clinics-closing-report"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 closed nearly a quarter of \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  clinics}}}\uc2  by 2016, a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2020/mar/18/missouri-last-abortion-clinic-decision-imminent"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tense battle}}}\uc2  over the single remaining clinic in Missouri \u8211\'20\'13 these three films are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://theconversation.com/in-2020-tv-and-film-still-couldnt-get-abortion-right-152223"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 promising outliers}}}\uc2  in depicting the reality of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the US, whose barriers to access remain {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/0959353516681245?journalCode=fapa"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  under-represented in film and TV}}}\uc2 . All three take, for once, the validity of a teenage girl\u8217\'20\'19s decision to end a pregnancy as a given; the dramatic tension is not whether the character will decide to go through with an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  but if she\u8217\'20\'19s able to given the hurdles, both comically inane and devastatingly prohibitive, to reproductive healthcare in the US.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s a welcome shift in focus for a topic long under-played or over-dramatized on-screen. Ever since the \uc2 CBS\uc2  sitcom Maude became the first primetime TV storyline to focus on a woman\u8217\'20\'19s decision to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1972, the year before Roe v Wade, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been a fraught topic for film and TV, often met with backlash. On TV, especially teen soaps such as Degrassi, Dawson\u8217\'20\'19s Creek, or Party of Five, the plot often hinged on the woman\u8217\'20\'19s decision, portrayed as anguished for herself and others. Most TV women who contemplated \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  eventually decided, at the last minute, to keep the baby (Dawson\u8217\'20\'19s Creek, Beverly Hills, 90210, The OC, which backed away from even using the word \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D on-air), even if they made it to a clinician (Sex and the City, Felicity), prompting the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 Kate Aurthur to call \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2004 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2004/07/18/arts/television-television-s-most-persistent-taboo.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Ctelevision\u8217\'20\'19s most persistent taboo\u8221\'20\'1D.}}}\par \par \uc2 The same was generally true for movies in the 90s and 2000s, which depicted the ending of unwanted pregnancy as agonizing, if it was considered it at all. Think Knocked Up, whose plot hinged on Katherine Heigl\u8217\'20\'19s type-A Alison not even considering ending an unintended pregnancy from a one-night stand, Waitress, or Oscar-nominated Juno, whose protagonist balks at an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  once a classmate dubiously informs her the fetus has fingernails.\par \par \uc2 In the past 15 years, storylines {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/arts-entertainment/2019/03/19/unsentimental-abortion-scene-shrill-isnt-groundbreaking-heres-why-thats-big-deal/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have shifted}}}\uc2  toward \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as an unchallenged, positive health decision for TV characters, ironically as restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers have abounded; of the 1,300 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions enacted in the nearly half-century since Roe v Wade, a stunning 43% were passed in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/may/25/anti-abortion-movement-bullish-us-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 just the last decade}}}\uc2 . It hasn\u8217\'20\'19t been without controversy \u8211\'20\'13 primetime \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  storylines in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vulture.com/2011/09/shonda_rhimes_talks_about_grey.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2011 episode of Grey\u8217\'20\'19s Anatomy}}}\uc2  and a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theatlantic.com/entertainment/archive/2010/07/friday-night-lights-tackles-abortion/59495/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2010 episode of Friday Night Lights}}}\uc2  (the rare episode to depict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions \u8211\'20\'13 in this case, Texas\u8217\'20\'19s mandatory waiting period for a 16-year-old character), were the subject of tense debate between show writers/creators and network Broadcast Standards and Practices.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s only in recent years, against the backdrop of a coordinated legislative crackdown on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, that what Jezebel has termed the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://jezebel.com/jane-the-virgins-xo-has-a-chill-abortion-1788241538"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cchill \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 \u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  depicted, accurately, as a medical decision personal to each woman \u8211\'20\'13 became unremarkable on TV. In Jane the Virgin season 3, for example, Jane\u8217\'20\'19s mother, Xo, a woman in her 40s, gets an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  following a romantic fling; the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/arts-entertainment/2019/03/19/unsentimental-abortion-scene-shrill-isnt-groundbreaking-heres-why-thats-big-deal/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 first episode of Hulu\u8217\'20\'19s Shrill}}}\uc2  finds Annie (Aidy Bryant) receiving an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Planned Parenthood, that\u8217\'20\'19s barely mentioned again. There were also relatively un-dramatic \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  plotlines on Scandal, Claws, GLOW, Sex Education, Dear White People, Euphoria and, a few years back, Girls.\par \par \uc2 While depictions of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have proliferated, to an extent, on television, film has been slower on the uptake, with the exception of last year; according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ansirh.org/research/abortion/pop-culture?media_type%5B0%5D=49"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Onscreen}}}\uc2 , a project run by reproductive health researchers at the \uc2 University of California at San Francisco\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ansirh.org/sites/default/files/field/page-files/abortiononscreenreport2020final.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 13 movies released in 2020}}}\uc2  depicted or discussed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  (compared to three in 2019), the most in a single year to date. (As in television, representation skewed disproportionately white, though women of color comprise the majority of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients in the US.) This includes Unpregnant and Never Rarely Sometimes Always, as well as Alex Thompson\u8217\'20\'19s independent film Saint Frances, which treats a 34-year-old deciding to end a surprise pregnancy with sensitivity that doesn\u8217\'20\'19t overshadow her complexity as a character \u8211\'20\'13 the guilt that Bridget (writer Kelly O\u8217\'20\'19Sullivan) feels is conflated with her career stagnancy and fluctuating self-worth. (An unvarnished scene of cramps and blood clots is also one of the very few film/TV depictions of medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , an increasingly common method for women in the US.)\par \par \uc2 Not all depictions skew even lightly pro-choice; the anti-choice propaganda film {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2019/mar/28/unplanned-review-anti-abortion-propaganda-is-a-gory-mess"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Unplanned}}}\uc2  featured a gory portrayal of a 13-week-old fetus \u8220\'20\'1Ctwisting and fighting for its life,\u8221\'20\'1D according to protagonist Abby \u8211\'20\'13 a depiction numerous medical experts {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2019/04/08/movies/unplanned-movie-abortion.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 deemed}}}\uc2  inaccurate and misleading. The film {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thecut.com/2019/04/unplanned-anti-abortion-movie.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sold out theaters}}}\uc2  across the country and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.boxofficemojo.com/release/rl436831745/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 grossed $19m}}}\uc2  domestically. The drama {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2021/mar/24/roe-v-wade-anti-abortion-rightwing"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Roe v Wade}}}\uc2 , produced by the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theringer.com/movies/2018/7/17/17580316/roe-v-wade-movie-conservative-nick-loeb-cathy-allyn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 siloed conservative Hollywood ecosystem}}}\uc2  and released this March, features numerous rightwing celebrities including Jon Voight, Stacey Dash and Tomi Lahren falsely discrediting the 1973 ruling.\par \par \uc2 The \u8220\'20\'1Cchill \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D TV trope works to neutralizes the hysteria of anti-choice scare tactics by normalizing the experience of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as uncomplicated and uncontested, but it\u8217\'20\'19s often {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://theconversation.com/in-2020-tv-and-film-still-couldnt-get-abortion-right-152223"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 out of step}}}\uc2  with the decidedly unchill hurdles to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the US, from cost to distance (it could be a half-day drive to the nearest clinic) to mandatory waiting periods. Unpregnant, Plan B, and Never Sometimes Rarely Always reflect an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/may/25/anti-abortion-movement-bullish-us-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 increasingly hostile}}}\uc2  landscape for reproductive rights in the US \u8211\'20\'13 since January, 15 state legislatures have enacted 70 restrictions, including 10 outright \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. Last month, Republicans in Texas passed a law that would allow a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kut.org/health/2021-05-19/texas-new-anti-abortion-law-could-be-tough-for-providers-to-challenge-in-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 private citizen to sue people}}}\uc2  who help others {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/apr/27/us-tiny-towns-anti-abortion-ordinances"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 obtain an \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  past six weeks}}}\uc2 . Twenty-nine states, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/fact-sheet/induced-abortion-united-states"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 where 58% of American women of reproductive age live}}}\uc2 , are considered hostile to reproductive rights. The supreme court is preparing to hear a case on behalf of Mississippi\u8217\'20\'19s sole remaining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic that presents the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/may/18/us-supreme-court-abortion-test-case-mississippi-precedent"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 most serious challenge to Roe}}}\uc2  in decades \u8211\'20\'13 a culmination of conservative extremism given outsize legislative import through {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/apr/09/us-voting-restrictions-conservative-groups-lobbying-against-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Republican-led voting restrictions and partisan gerrymandering}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That broader context doesn\u8217\'20\'19t explicitly enter the frame in these films, save for a rousing, exasperated speech \u8211\'20\'13 \u8220\'20\'1Cwhy the hell do you need to get parental consent to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  but not to actually birth a human child? Fuck you, Missouri state legislature!\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 delivered in Unpregnant by Veronica, who has (unrealistically) endured a car chase. Instead, all three films hew closely to the characters\u8217\'20\'19 bafflement and frustration, as well as the mundanity and well-researched specifics of reproductive care once the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  appointment / emergency contraceptive is finally secured. Never Rarely Sometimes Always takes it name from the prescribed answers on a clinical intake form at a real Planned Parenthood in Manhattan, whose questions \u8211\'20\'13 \u8220\'20\'1Chas anyone made you have sex against your will?\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 do much more to reveal Autumn\u8217\'20\'19s internal conflict and pain than any line justifying her decision.\par \par \uc2 For the most part, these characters want what most teenage girls do: to feel safe, to feel seen, to get on with their lives. As she searches for the morning-after pill, Sunny is furious, flustered, scared, confused; she\u8217\'20\'19s also annoyed, hungry, pissed off at her best friend for convincing her to host a party and crushing on a cute guy from class. Autumn and Skylar are miserable, vulnerable, terrified, and also delighted by a bakery in the big city, buoyed by trust in each other. Bailey and Veronica scream along to Since U Been Gone and patch together a tattered friendship. It\u8217\'20\'19s a romp with a pointed barb: if even one of the status quo\u8217\'20\'19s primary beneficiaries \u8211\'20\'13 a white girl like Veronica, affluent (and conservative) parents on speed-dial \u8211\'20\'13 struggles to get a chill \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , things are probably deranged.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s too early to tell whether the reproductive roadtrip film boom is a fluke of timing or a genuine shift in perspective, and opportunities for female writers/directors. But it\u8217\'20\'19s clear that in 2021, the barriers to reproductive healthcare in the US are so much a part of the American context that it\u8217\'20\'19s malleable into three distinct films \u8211\'20\'13 and with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/may/18/us-supreme-court-abortion-test-case-mississippi-precedent"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a likely bitter fight ahead}}}\uc2 , there\u8217\'20\'19s plenty of material for a burgeoning genre of films placing the burden of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  not on female characters, but the healthcare system made unduly hostile to them.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 This article was amended on 4 June 2021 to clarify that Maude was the first primetime TV show to focus on a woman\u8217\'20\'19s decision to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , not the first TV storyline about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210603eh63000p6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
