\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 At least 10 states divert federal welfare funding to anti- \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  clinics\b0\par\par\uc2 Jessica Glenza \par \uc2 1,440 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 June 2021\par 15:20 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Millions in aid intended to go to the neediest families is being used to finance clinics trying to dissuade women from having abortions\par \par \uc2 At least 10 US states have siphoned millions of dollars from federal block grants, meant to provide aid to their neediest families, to pay for the operations of ideological anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics.\par \par \uc2 These overwhelmingly Republican-led states used money from the federal Temporary Assistance for Needy Families program (Tanf), better known as welfare or direct cash aid, to fund the activities of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics associated with the evangelical right. The clinics work to dissuade women from obtaining abortions.\par \par \uc2 In all cases, the states used these funds even as Covid-19 caused the worst economic upheaval in nearly a century, left {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2021/apr/14/americas-year-of-hunger-how-children-and-people-of-color-suffered-most"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one in four families}}}\uc2  without enough to eat, and resulted in mass layoffs that had a disproportionate effect on low-income and racial minority Americans.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re not a replacement for Tanf, by any stretch of the imagination,\u8221\'20\'1D said Andrea Swartzendruber, an associate professor of epidemiology and biostatistics at the University of Georgia College of Public Health, whose research has focused on how crisis pregnancy centers operate.\par \par \uc2 Indiana, Louisiana, Michigan, Missouri, North Carolina, North Dakota, Ohio, Oklahoma, Pennsylvania and Texas have used federal Tanf funds to support anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics.\par \par \uc2 The revelation is to be published in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://equityfwd.org/research/mapping-deception-closer-look-how-states-anti-abortion-center-programs-operate"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report by the accountability group Equity Forward}}}\uc2 , which was provided exclusively to the Guardian. Equity Forward produces investigative research on human rights, gender equity and sexual and reproductive health and justice.\par \par \uc2 These 10 states funneled the money through \u8220\'20\'1CAlternatives to \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D programs, part of state budgets established by conservative legislatures and often run through state health departments. They not only send millions in federal welfare funds, but also state taxpayer dollars to such centers.\par \par \uc2 Clinton-era welfare reforms caused, \u8220\'20\'1Ca fundamental shift in how cash assistance to families with children with little or no income was funded\u8221\'20\'1D, said Ife Floyd, a senior policy analyst at the Center on Budget and Policy Priorities.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CStates have control over those dollars in a way they didn\u8217\'20\'19t necessarily have before,\u8221\'20\'1D Floyd added.\par \par \uc2 The result has been increasingly dramatic variation in how states spent the federal dollars. States, especially in the south, have imposed harsh eligibility requirements in debates {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbpp.org/blog/family-cash-assistance-programs-marked-by-historical-racism-especially-in-south"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 marred by racism}}}\uc2 , and pushed people off benefits even in times of economic turmoil, such as the Great Recession and the Covid-19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 As the 2001 book {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.yorku.ca/crsp/issue51/issue51-14.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Welfare Racism}}}\uc2  described it, legislators developed a \u8220\'20\'1Cpreoccupation with controlling the alleged sexual immorality and supposed preference of welfare over work of one group: African-American females\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Missouri is one such example of how welfare has withered. In 2015, the state passed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.senate.mo.gov/15info/BTS_Web/Bill.aspx?SessionType=R&BillID=153"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 draconian welfare reforms}}}\uc2  that imposed a 45-month lifetime limit for cash assistance, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://cssp.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/Racist-Roots-of-Work-Requirements-CSSP-1.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 work requirements}}}\uc2  and sanctions for people who did not comply.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve created a new class of Missourians,\u8221\'20\'1D Glenn Koenen, a hunger adviser with the left-leaning group Empower Missouri, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://themissouritimes.com/welfare-reform-bill-force-30000-off-food-stamps/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said at the time the reforms were passed}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CWe now have legislated that some of our neighbors are too poor to get help from anti-poverty programs.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Between 1 January 2016, when the reform went into effect, and April 2021, more than 71% of beneficiaries dropped off Missouri\u8217\'20\'19s program. That included 28,643 children and 16,942 families.\par \par \uc2 Missouri then spent funds not paid to families on other programs, among them the Alternatives to \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  program. Since 2017, it has sent $26m to anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics, according to state budgets. The average monthly benefit for a Missouri family is $256.\par \par \uc2 The program gained traction after it was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.realalternatives.org/Press-Kit/History-PF.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pioneered in 2001}}}\uc2  by the former Pennsylvania senator Rick Santorum, who also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/fact-checker/post/rick-santorum-and-welfare-reform-fact-checker-biography/2012/01/12/gIQAmmUTtP_blog.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 helped craft the 1990s welfare reforms}}}\uc2  that made such state spending possible. That year, Pennsylvania became the first state to divert Tanf funds to anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics, and sent $1m to its Alternatives to \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  program.\par \par \uc2 The program was then popularized with help from the Pennsylvania-based anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic Real Alternatives, whose representatives went on to \u8220\'20\'1Ceducate\u8221\'20\'1D other anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  organizations about how to obtain state funding.\par \par \uc2 Conservative states then replicated the program as welfare reform passed out of the public spotlight, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions proved a winning strategy for Republicans, and anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics lobbied for more funds. Ohio Right to Life, for example, said it {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ohiolife.org/parenting_and_pregnancy_support_program"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cpursued\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  federal welfare funds for three years in a row.\par \par \uc2 While there is no public accounting of all the federal funds anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics have received in the last two decades, Texas\u8217\'20\'19s large program sheds light on the sheer volume of cash.\par \par \uc2 Since 2006, Texas has sent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hhs.texas.gov/sites/default/files/documents/laws-regulations/reports-presentations/2020/hb1-alternatives-abortion-dec-2020.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $45m}}}\uc2  in federal welfare funds to crisis pregnancy centers. Texas has extraordinarily strict income limits for its welfare program \u8211\'20\'13 a family of four can earn no more than {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hhs.texas.gov/services/financial/cash/tanf-cash-help"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $231 per month}}}\uc2  to be eligible.\par \par \uc2 Conservative lawmakers have argued anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics provide assistance to the needy by helping women who unexpectedly find themselves pregnant or in \u8220\'20\'1Ccrisis\u8221\'20\'1D. They are targeted to the young, low-income and people of color. Driven by an ideological agenda, they universally oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and are often affiliated with large evangelical or Catholic networks.\par \par \uc2 However, many experts have described crisis pregnancy centers as \u8220\'20\'1Cunethical\u8221\'20\'1D and deceptive, because they pose as medical facilities and offer \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6318184/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 medically unnecessary}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D ultrasounds. Most often, medical services do not go beyond tests for sexually transmitted infections.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t think the concern is clinics that offer alternatives to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D said the former Missouri representative Deb Lavender, who in 2017 introduced a bill to stop Missouri from giving welfare funds directly to crisis pregnancy centers. \u8220\'20\'1CThe concern is that they lie to women and they trap women\u8221\'20\'1D through omission or misinformation, she said.\par \par \uc2 Others may encourage women to wait to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , exaggerate the risk of miscarriage, or draw spurious links between \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and breast cancer, depression and future infertility.\par \par \uc2 Outside of STI tests, what crisis pregnancy centers provide most often are diapers, wipes, clothes and perhaps car seats, said Swartzendruber. But those items come with a cost.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople, in order to get those goods and materials, have to basically earn them with their time and effort \u8211\'20\'13 whether it\u8217\'20\'19s watching videos [or] going to Bible studies,\u8221\'20\'1D Swartzendruber said. \u8220\'20\'1CSomeone in need could not expect to show up to a crisis pregnancy center and just get diapers. They have to be earned over time.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 State funding streams have also resulted in a dramatic imbalance in the number of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics versus full-service reproductive health providers, who are under near-constant attack.\par \par \uc2 Today, there are more than 2,500 ideologically focused, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics, compared with just 800 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers. But those figures don\u8217\'20\'19t tell the whole story, as they are dramatically skewed in states hostile to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 In some states, the balance is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.prochoiceamerica.org/wp-content/uploads/2017/04/cpc-report-2015.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  provider to 15 anti-\b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  clinics}}}\uc2 . In Missouri, that balance is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://dss.mo.gov/dfas/taxcredit/pdf/qualified-pregnancy-resource-centers.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one to 74}}}\uc2 , as the state has worked aggressively to shut down its only remaining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic, a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2019/may/28/missouri-abortion-clinic-closure-first-state-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Planned Parenthood in St Louis}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Many states have chosen to divert these funds amid chronic budget shortfalls, shield centers from public scrutiny and allow contractors to conduct their own oversight.\par \par \uc2 In 2017, Oklahoma ranked number one in cuts to education and female incarceration. Nevertheless, the state chose to continue to divert Tanf funds to crisis pregnancy centers even amid {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2017/aug/29/oklahoma-education-system-four-day-school-weeks-poor"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 chronic budget deficits}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Advocates have also been blocked in their attempts to understand how crisis pregnancy centers work with state agencies. Missouri has attempted to charge Equity Forward {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.columbiatribune.com/story/news/state/2021/03/17/group-seeking-public-records-missouri-alternative-abortion-program-charged-26-k-sunshine-week/4735909001/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $26,219}}}\uc2  for an open records request for emails.\par \par \uc2 Often, crisis pregnancy centers used federal welfare funds and state taxpayer dollars with little oversight. In North Dakota and Oklahoma, the only oversight provision in contracts states crisis pregnancy centers must be \u8220\'20\'1Coutcome-driven with positive results\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Pennsylvania\u8217\'20\'19s Alternatives to \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  program gave $30m to Real Alternatives alone from state and federal funds from 2012 to 2017. Real Alternatives then used that money to set up pregnancy centers in Michigan, using \u8220\'20\'1Chundreds of thousands of Pennsylvania taxpayer dollars to fund its activities in other states\u8221\'20\'1D, Pennsylvania\u8217\'20\'19s auditor general, Eugene DePasquale, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.paauditor.gov/press-releases/auditor-general-depasquale-says-audit-stopped-abuse-of-state-funds-by-abortion-alternative-provider-real-alternatives"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said in}}}\uc2  2017.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is just as outrageous that the state grant agreement was so weak that it allowed this practice to go on for decades siphoning funds intended to benefit Pennsylvania women experiencing crisis pregnancies,\u8221\'20\'1D the auditor general said.\par \par \uc2 Despite the problems these programs have encountered, their popularity continues to grow among conservative legislators. Arkansas, Arizona and Iowa all sought to establish Alternatives to \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  programs in 2021, even amid the pandemic-devastated economy.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210604eh64001be\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
