\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Anti- \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  movement bullish as legal campaign reaches \uc2 US supreme court\b0\par\par\uc2 Jessica Glenza \par \uc2 1,317 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 May 2021\par 16:11 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 A case that could undermine the landmark Roe v Wade ruling and a punitive Texas law are the culmination of a decades-long push\par \par \uc2 The anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement in the US is emboldened and optimistic after the supreme court announced it would hear a direct challenge to laws underpinning the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the US, and Texas enacted a law intended to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks.\par \par \uc2 The high court decision to take up the case and the Texas move come during the most hostile year for reproductive rights in the nearly half-century since pregnant people won the constitutional right to choose whether to terminate a pregnancy in the landmark 1973 case Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe long-predicted scaling back of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights by the supreme court just got a lot more likely,\u8221\'20\'1D said Mary Ziegler, a legal historian, author of \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Law in America: Roe v Wade to the Present, and law professor at Florida State University.\par \par \uc2 Today, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal in all 50 states up to the point the fetus can survive outside the womb, a legal concept called \u8220\'20\'1Cviability\u8221\'20\'1D established in Roe. This is generally understood to be about 24 weeks (a full-term pregnancy is 39 weeks).\par \par \uc2 The case taken up by the court, called Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization, will answer whether Mississippi can limit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to 15 weeks, and is brought by the state\u8217\'20\'19s last \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic. If upheld, it would reduce by more than two months the time in which a woman could choose to terminate a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s really hard to see why the court would take this case unless they\u8217\'20\'19re interested in reversing part of Roe or all of Roe,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ziegler. Further, the court chose to answer \u8220\'20\'1Cthe most explosive question in the case\u8221\'20\'1D, which \u8220\'20\'1Csuggests they\u8217\'20\'19re not really worried about the political fallout\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 On the right, the hopes are clear: that the court will end the legal right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and potentially allow room to criminalize the procedure.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re all hopeful the court will be intellectually honest and acknowledge what the science is clear on \u8211\'20\'13 that a unique human life starts at fertilization,\u8221\'20\'1D said Lila Rose, founder and president of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocacy group Life Action. Rose is widely seen as the face of the millennial anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement.\par \par \uc2 Mississippi is just one of 29 states across the south and midwest considered hostile to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/fact-sheet/induced-abortion-united-states"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 where 58% of American women of reproductive age live}}}\uc2 , and which would probably act to further restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 The supreme court case represents the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/may/18/us-supreme-court-abortion-test-case-mississippi-precedent"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 most severe challenge ever presented to Roe}}}\uc2 , and is a reflection of how the country has splintered in a decade of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/apr/09/us-voting-restrictions-conservative-groups-lobbying-against-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Republican-led voting restrictions and partisan gerrymandering}}}\uc2 , the process of redrawing politicians\u8217\'20\'19 districts to favor one party.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re becoming two countries, and your voting rights and your reproductive rights are increasingly likely to depend on where you live,\u8221\'20\'1D said David Daley, a senior fellow at \uc2 FairVote\uc2  and the bestselling author of Rat F**ked: Why Your Vote Doesn\u8217\'20\'19t Count.\par \par \uc2 Historians and analysts drew a direct line from the 2010 Tea Party sweep of state legislatures, which allowed Republicans to redraw districts in their favor, to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2019/jun/18/us-anti-abortion-bans-backlash-gerrymandering"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 increasingly extreme and unconstitutional anti-\b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  laws}}}\uc2 , such as the Mississippi law scheduled to be heard by the supreme court.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CRedistricting and extreme partisan gerrymandering creates non-competitive districts where all of the action moves from the general election to the primary,\u8221\'20\'1D said Daley. Primaries, he said, \u8220\'20\'1Ctend to be low-turnout, off-season elections dominated by the most extreme voices\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 A remarkably stable majority of Americans support upholding Roe. Only {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/19/briefing/abortion-debate-public-opinion.html?action=click&module=Briefings&pgtype=Homepage"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 about 20%}}}\uc2  of Americans believe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be illegal in all circumstances.\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, 2021 has proved to be the most hostile year for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights on record. Since January, more than 70 restrictions have been enacted in 15 states, including 10 outright \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. Not coincidentally, the previous most hostile year was 2011, when Republicans gained wide control of statehouses, according to the reproductive rights organization the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C[Republicans] have not lost a single chamber in any of those states over the last decade, even in years when Democratic candidates win hundreds of thousands more votes than Republican candidates,\u8221\'20\'1D said Daley.\par \par \uc2 Exactly 1,300 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions have been enacted since 1973, when Roe was decided, with 553 coming in the last decade. In other words, it took nearly 40 years to pass 57% of restrictions, and only a decade to pass the rest.\par \par \uc2 Rose describes this accumulation of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions as a \u8220\'20\'1Cshift in our country on the rights and the value of the pre-born child\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Daley said there was \u8220\'20\'1Cno evidence\u8221\'20\'1D for Rose\u8217\'20\'19s assertion that more people are becoming pro-life, but there is evidence of increasingly extreme dynamics playing out in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2020/aug/21/gerrymandering-republicans-us-election-structural-racism"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 host of other issues}}}\uc2 , as Republicans block popular policies such as reforms to gun laws, increases in the minimum wage and expanding the franchise.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s entirely a symptom of the extreme partisan gerrymandering,\u8221\'20\'1D said Daley.\par \par \uc2 For anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  organizations, some of which are now putting {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/apr/09/us-voting-restrictions-conservative-groups-lobbying-against-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 multimillion-dollar lobbying bets on restricting voting}}}\uc2 , the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision to take the Dobbs case \u8220\'20\'1Creconfirmed the importance of some of these strategies\u8221\'20\'1D, said Ziegler.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe gameplan became to turn out the people who care the most and discourage other people from voting, and not worry about whether the median voter liked what you were doing any more,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ziegler.\par \par \uc2 The result is an increasingly wide divide among states in the south and midwest, most of which are hostile to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and states along the coasts which have girded the legal underpinnings of the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and explored new ways to help women pay for it.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COftentimes, those places become hubs for folks in neighboring states to get care,\u8221\'20\'1D said Destiny Lopez, co-president of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/AllAboveAll?ref_src=twsrc%5Egoogle%7Ctwcamp%5Eserp%7Ctwgr%5Eauthor"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 All* Above All}}}\uc2 , an advocacy group which has worked in both progressive and conservative states to expand \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople kind of see the issue right now as you either have \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or you don\u8217\'20\'19t. Roe is legal or illegal, and it\u8217\'20\'19s actually much more nuanced than that,\u8221\'20\'1D said Lopez, because the right to terminate a pregnancy has always been more difficult for some people to access.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThose people tend to be black and brown, they tend to be younger, they tend to be immigrants,\u8221\'20\'1D Lopez said. \u8220\'20\'1CPeople who are working to make ends meet.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In Texas, where sweeping voting rights restrictions have been led by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Republicans, the state\u8217\'20\'19s move last week meant it became the first to allow a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kut.org/health/2021-05-19/texas-new-anti-abortion-law-could-be-tough-for-providers-to-challenge-in-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 private citizen to sue people}}}\uc2  who help others {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/apr/27/us-tiny-towns-anti-abortion-ordinances"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 obtain an \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  past six weeks gestation}}}\uc2 . Pro-choice groups are exploring ways to challenge the law, which would come into effect in September.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe bill grants virtually anyone the right to sue a person helping a pregnant person seeking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D said Amanda Thayer, a spokesperson for Naral Pro-Choice America, one of the country\u8217\'20\'19s most influential reproductive rights advocacy groups.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDo we want to live in a world where your Uber driver could be sued for driving someone to access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care?\u8221\'20\'1D Thayer asked.\par \par \uc2 What is certain is the court\u8217\'20\'19s decision will have a profound political impact. A decision will probably be issued in June 2022, a few months before the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 There are also signs the case is controversial within the court. Justices rescheduled sessions on whether to take the case for months, a highly unusual practice. Four of the nine justices on the court must elect to hear a case to bring it before the court.\par \par \uc2 But, Ziegler said, \u8220\'20\'1CYou\u8217\'20\'19re not going to get four justices taking a case unless they feel they can get that fifth vote.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210525eh5p001xj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
