\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 The Texas  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ban is a performance of misogyny. But it might get worse\b0\par\par\uc2 Moira Donegan \par \uc2 1,140 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 May 2021\par 17:26 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Unconstitutional anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws are often a grim kind of misogynist political theater. But that might be changing\par \par \uc2 Senate Bill 8, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/19/us/texas-abortion-law.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 six-week \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ban}}}\uc2  that the Texas governor, Greg Abbott, signed into law last week, is a total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in everything but name. The bill is one of several across the country that bans abortions at six weeks of gestation \u8211\'20\'13 in layman\u8217\'20\'19s terms, four weeks after fertilization and two weeks after the first missed period.\par \par \uc2 Texas is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/state-policy/explore/state-policies-later-abortions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the ninth state}}}\uc2  to pass such a bill, named by the anti-choice movement that lobbies for them as \u8220\'20\'1Cfetal heartbeat bills\u8221\'20\'1D. The term is a misnomer, because at six weeks of gestation there is neither a fetus nor a heartbeat. Indeed, there is no heart. At six weeks, the pregnancy consists of an embryo, which will not develop into a fetus for nearly another month. No heart, and no other organ, is present. The so-called \u8220\'20\'1Cheartbeat\u8221\'20\'1D that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents refer to is actually the pulsing of some cells that are starting to specialize, and which will eventually form cardiac tissue if the pregnancy continues. At the phase of pregnancy when abortions are banned by the new Texas legislation, the embryo is about the size of a pea. There are no exceptions for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 The bill amounts to a near total ban precisely because of how early it cuts off legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . At that stage in a pregnancy, most women don\u8217\'20\'19t yet know that they are pregnant. Even those who do sometimes can\u8217\'20\'19t access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care that early, as providers often prefer to wait until eight or 10 weeks of gestation to perform abortions, for safety reasons. Before that stage, it is difficult to rule out the possibility of an ectopic pregnancy, an unviable condition that can be fatal.\par \par \uc2 The Texas bill is set to go into effect in September, but the implementation of its central provision, the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, is almost certain to be delayed as the law is challenged in federal court. This has been the fate of all eight of its predecessor bills, which have been delayed or thrown out by federal courts after being passed in Georgia, Iowa, Kentucky, Louisiana, Mississippi, North Dakota, Ohio, South Carolina and Tennessee. The same has been true for a number of other bills meant to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at other pre-viability stages of pregnancy: eight weeks, 10 weeks, 12 weeks, 18 weeks.\par \par \uc2 For years, Republican controlled states have always passed these bills, and federal courts have always thrown them out, even those courts in districts where the federal judiciary is packed with arch-conservatives and anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ideologues. There has simply not been a legal rationale for upholding them. The supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s precedent that outright \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans pre-viability are unconstitutional \u8211\'20\'13 established in 1973 with Roe v Wade and reaffirmed in 1992 with Planned Parenthood v Casey \u8211\'20\'13 is simply too unambiguous.\par \par \uc2 If Republicans know that these state laws are going to get thrown out by the federal courts, why have they kept on passing them? Even without formal implementation, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans accomplish a lot for the anti-choice movement. They frighten and confuse pregnant women, and they further stigmatize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They prove useful messaging and fundraising tools for state Republican politicians. And they set up test cases for the federal courts, allowing the anti-choice movement to workshop new legal theories and tactics for narrowing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and harassing providers out of business.\par \par \uc2 Even without formal implementation, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans accomplish a lot for the anti-choice movement. They frighten and confuse pregnant women, and they further stigmatize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s in this last element that the Texas law represents an innovation for the misogynist right. In addition to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, the bill includes a peculiar provision that privatizes the enforcement of that law. SB8 grants standing to any individual \u8211\'20\'13 including those outside the state \u8211\'20\'13 to sue people in Texas who \u8220\'20\'1Caid or abet\u8221\'20\'1D an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or who \u8220\'20\'1Cintend\u8221\'20\'1D to help an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patient. Like {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.plannedparenthoodaction.org/issues/abortion/trap-laws"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trap laws}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 targeted restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, a separate slate of state level anti-choice laws that are designed to make it too expensive and burdensome for providers to perform abortions within a state \u8211\'20\'13 this part of the law seems largely intended at punishing doctors and nurses, increasing provider overhead costs, and ultimately shutting down clinics. According to the bill, those who sue can collect a minimum of $10,000 if they win. But if a defendant prevails in the lawsuit, they are not able to recoup legal fees.\par \par \uc2 But in addition to targeting providers, the civil lawsuit provision of the Texas bill is so broadly written as to amount to a large-scale attack on any kind of action or speech in support of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. It would render virtually everyone involved with the operation of a clinic, or who materially contributes to a pro-choice organization, to be sued. Clinic escort volunteers can be sued; so can non-medical clinic staff, like janitors or receptionists. Anyone who has donated to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund, or a pro-choice organization such as Planned Parenthood, can be sued under the Texas law. So can anyone who provides any kind of material support to a patient seeking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as a ride to or from the clinic. After an outcry, the Texas legislature amended the bill to create an exception, saying that a rapist would not be permitted to use the law to sue the providers who gave his victim her \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But the law only applies to those men who have been convicted of rape. In Texas, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://gov.texas.gov/uploads/files/organization/criminal-justice/Sexual-Assault-Survivors-Task-Force-Biennial-Report_November-2020.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 91% of rapes}}}\uc2  go unreported.\par \par \uc2 If left intact, the law would not only force Texas women to remain pregnant against their will; it would also empower any misogynist or anti-choice person to impose their bigotry on Texas residents through frivolous and harassing lawsuits. Hopefully, courts will throw out the civil suit provision. If they don\u8217\'20\'19t, free speech in Texas will be severely curtailed.\par \par \uc2 For the most part, these bills have functioned as a grim kind of misogynist political theater, deliberate messaging exercises that don\u8217\'20\'19t really go anywhere. But that might be changing. Last week, the supreme court agreed to hear a case challenging a Mississippi law that bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 15 weeks of gestation. The central question in that case will be whether pre-viability \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans really are constitutional. If the court rules in favor of Mississippi \u8211\'20\'13 and they appear likely to \u8211\'20\'13 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans like the one that Texas just passed will become legal. And the falsely named \u8220\'20\'1Cheartbeat bills\u8221\'20\'1D will go from a performance of misogyny, to an enforcement of it.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Moira Donegan is a Guardian US columnist\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210523eh5n000rv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
