\par\par \uc2 Law\par \b \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s revenge: tilting of supreme court to the right poised to bear fruit\b0\par\par\uc2 David Smith in Washington \par \uc2 1,334 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 May 2021\par 06:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Cases on voting rights, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and gun control will be a test of the ex-president\u8217\'20\'19s laser-like focus on reshaping the judiciary\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s presidency was capricious and chaotic, but there was one issue on which he focused with laser-like discipline: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2018/may/30/donald-trump-judges-courts-justice-system"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tilting the judiciary to the right.}}}\par \par \uc2 Now America is about to reap that harvest. In the next year the supreme court is set to consider healthcare, voting, LGBTQ rights, guns and, most explosively, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The cases provide a vivid demonstration of how, after being rejected at the ballot box, conservative partisans could push their agenda through the courts instead.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNext year\u8217\'20\'19s supreme court term is shaping up to be the revenge of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D said {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://law.marquette.edu/faculty-and-staff-directory/detail/1996511"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Edward Fallone}}}\uc2 , an associate professor at Marquette University Law School.\par \par \uc2 It was always said that, long after the tweets and leaks were forgotten, Trump\u8217\'20\'19s judicial legacy would endure. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ballotpedia.org/Federal_judges_nominated_by_Donald_Trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 He appointed 234 judges,}}}\uc2  including 54 appellate judges, outpacing \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s first term total of 172 and \uc2 George W Bush\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s 204.\par \par \uc2 The blitz included three supreme court justices, most recently Amy Coney Barrett, a devout Catholic, who replaced \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  following the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/sep/23/ruth-bader-ginsburg-supreme-court-mourning"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 liberal linchpin\u8217\'20\'19s death,}}}\uc2  handing conservatives a 6-3 majority.\par \par \uc2 Just after Barrett\u8217\'20\'19s arrival and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s victory, the court heard arguments in a new challenge to \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s signature healthcare law, the Affordable Care Act. A decision is expected soon. If the court\u8217\'20\'19s conservatives overturn Obamacare, they would succeed where Republicans in Congress failed.\par \par \uc2 That outcome is seen as unlikely, but the right is expected to fare better in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vox.com/22286213/supreme-court-voting-rights-act-arizona-brnovich-democratic-national-committee-republican-party"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 two cases brought by Democrats}}}\uc2  contending that voting restrictions in Arizona are racially discriminatory. A ruling, expected soon, could make it harder to challenge dozens of other Republican-led voting measures in the wake of last year\u8217\'20\'19s election.\par \par \uc2 Another looming case involves a Roman Catholic adoption agency in Philadelphia that argues it is entitled to discriminate against potential foster parents on the basis of sexual orientation. Arguments at a hearing last November again implied that the conservative majority will rule in favour of the agency.\par \par \uc2 The supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s 2020-21 term offers further flashpoints. The nine justices will {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2021/04/26/us/supreme-court-gun.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 review a challenge}}}\uc2  to New York\u8217\'20\'19s restrictions on people carrying concealed handguns in public. It will be the court\u8217\'20\'19s first major gun rights case in more than a decade, even as Biden pushes for Congress to tackle America\u8217\'20\'19s firearm violence epidemic.\par \par \uc2 And, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2021/may/17/abortion-case-supreme-court-threatens-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 it emerged this week,}}}\uc2  America\u8217\'20\'19s highest court will also consider a bid to revive a Republican-backed state law that would ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks of pregnancy. The eventual ruling could undercut the seminal 1973 Roe v Wade decision that enshrined a woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide.\par \par \uc2 Fallone believes the move is a sign that the court\u8217\'20\'19s conservatives are no longer worried about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2020/jun/30/john-roberts-supreme-court-conservatives"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Chief Justice John Roberts}}}\uc2  casting a decisive vote against them. \u8220\'20\'1CThe only reason to take this case would be to overturn Roe,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re pretty confident they can succeed without it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Despite holding the White House and both chambers of Congress, Democrats have few options in the short term. Fallone, who {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.falloneforjustice.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unsuccessfully ran for the Wisconsin supreme court}}}\uc2  as a Democrat, added: \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t really think realistically that there is much that the Democrats can do about the court. The conservative majority is going to be emboldened next term.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19ve been waiting for that moment for a long time and it\u8217\'20\'19s finally going to arise. The second amendment case involving the New York law against carrying firearms openly in public is also setting up next term to be a big win for conservatives potentially as well.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A commission established by Biden to study potential changes to the supreme court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/business/legal/bidens-supreme-court-commission-meet-abortion-debate-reignites-2021-05-19/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 held its first meeting}}}\uc2  on Wednesday. It has six months to issue a report on reforms including possibly expanding the number of justices to 13, an idea championed by some liberal activists and Democratic members of Congress.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://demandjustice.org/christopher-kang/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Christopher Kang}}}\uc2 , co-founder and chief counsel of the pressure group Demand Justice, said: \u8220\'20\'1CFrom the gun violence prevention case to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case, the Republican super majority on the supreme court is showing that it is full steam ahead with its ultra-conservative agenda regardless of what the country thinks.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CUltimately we have to grapple with the need for structural reform of the supreme court. We\u8217\'20\'19re just starting to build the education and activism around this and gaining momentum in Congress. As the supreme court continues to take these grossly political steps, unfortunately they\u8217\'20\'19re going to make the argument for us about why the supreme court needs to be reformed.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A bill to expand the court was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://judiciary.house.gov/news/documentsingle.aspx?DocumentID=4508"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 introduced in Congress}}}\uc2  last month but Democratic moderates have expressed scepticism. Kang insisted: \u8220\'20\'1CSooner or later, this is going to be a question that comes to the Democrats about how we\u8217\'20\'19re going to preserve our democracy. The need to add supreme court seats, regrettably, is going to become very clear within the next couple of years.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Republicans have opposed the idea of expanding the number of justices, sometimes described as \u8220\'20\'1Ccourt packing\u8221\'20\'1D and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/history/2020/09/24/fdr-supreme-court-packing-rbg-trump/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 last seriously attempted }}}\uc2  by the Democratic president \uc2 Franklin Roosevelt\uc2  in the 1930s. They deny that they are using the courts as a workaround to subvert democracy and impose a form of minority rule.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.committeeforjustice.org/curt-levey"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Curt Levey}}}\uc2 , president of the rightwing advocacy group the Committee for Justice, said: \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t see Biden\u8217\'20\'19s narrow victory as an endorsement of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on demand, which is pretty much the current regime or, for that matter, as an endorsement of draconian restrictions on handguns. So I guess I don\u8217\'20\'19t really see it as being out of step.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think the opposite was true for many decades where the supreme court represented elite opinion that was out of touch with the majority. Perhaps for the first time, the supreme court is more in line with the American people generally. I certainly understand why the left is upset that they\u8217\'20\'19ve lost an institution that helped them to implement their agenda.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In fact Biden won the national popular vote by 7m ballots. About six in 10 Americans believe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or most cases, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/fact-tank/2021/05/06/about-six-in-ten-americans-say-abortion-should-be-legal-in-all-or-most-cases/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to the Pew Research Center,}}}\uc2  although the partisan split over the issue has deepened in recent years.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/importantmeagan?ref_src=twsrc%5Egoogle%7Ctwcamp%5Eserp%7Ctwgr%5Eauthor"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Meagan Hatcher-Mays,}}}\uc2  director of democracy policy at the grassroots movement Indivisible, said: \u8220\'20\'1CMost people in the United States think that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal and easy to access so it\u8217\'20\'19s not really a political winner for Republicans, especially among suburban women, to go at women\u8217\'20\'19s bodily autonomy like this legislatively.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf the courts do it, then they get what they want and they don\u8217\'20\'19t face any blowback for it because it wasn\u8217\'20\'19t them that did it. They didn\u8217\'20\'19t pass a law that says \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is bad; they got the supreme court to do it. And so that\u8217\'20\'19s what this 40-year project has been about: stacking the courts with people that are conservative loyalists and who will do the unpopular dirty work of Republicans that they can\u8217\'20\'19t get done legislatively.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It is a very long game. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theatlantic.com/ideas/archive/2021/04/trump-circuit-court-judges/618533/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 A recent article}}}\uc2  in the Atlantic magazine noted that Trump\u8217\'20\'19s judges will not reach the peak of their influence until the early 2040s, when they are likely to lead nearly every appeals court at the same time.\par \par \uc2 Hatcher-Mays warned: \u8220\'20\'1CThe lower courts also have been hijacked, frankly, by people who are loyal not just to conservative values but Republican political outcomes. That was Trump\u8217\'20\'19s big legacy. Not every case goes to the supreme court; a lot of them are decided at the lower level; a lot of those people that he picked are Trumpian-type judges. So that\u8217\'20\'19s really, really scary.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210523eh5n000dy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
