\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Guam moves closer to restoring  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  access after \uc2 ACLU\uc2  lawsuit\b0\par\par\uc2 Michelle Broder Van Dyke in Honolulu \par \uc2 674 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 March 2021\par 08:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s been impossible to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the island since 2018, and the closest legal clinic is in Hawai?i\par \par \uc2 Guam has taken a significant step toward restoring \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, after the \uc2 ACLU\uc2  scored a victory in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/feb/22/guam-abortions-aclu-lawsuit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a lawsuit}}}\uc2  that seeks to ensure residents of the US territory can turn to remote healthcare providers for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication.\par \par \uc2 Getting an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Guam has been impossible since 2018, when the last \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctor retired and moved off the island. Before then, at least 200 abortions occurred on Guam every year. Today, accessing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/feb/22/guam-abortions-aclu-lawsuit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the closest legal \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  clinic}}}\uc2  requires a flight to Hawai?i, an expensive and difficult undertaking especially during a pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Telemedicine medication abortions, which allow doctors to remotely prescribe the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drugs mifepristone and misoprostol to patients up to 10 weeks pregnant, would be an obvious solution, if it were not for a 1978 Guam law that says abortions must be \u8220\'20\'1Cperformed\u8221\'20\'1D in a \u8220\'20\'1Cmedical clinic or hospital\u8221\'20\'1D. The \uc2 ACLU\uc2  lawsuit is fighting, in part, to block that law from being enforced.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe know that many people have reached out to physicians in Hawai?i about accessing abortions and that not all of them have been able to overcome the enormous financial and logistical obstacles to traveling several thousand miles,\u8221\'20\'1D said Vanessa Williams, a Guam lawyer working with the \uc2 ACLU\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Historically, it has not been easy to access abortions on Guam despite them being legal, due to stigma and past restrictive laws. In the 1990s, the mostly Catholic US territory, where 165,000 people live, passed a law that prohibited \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  except when the mother\u8217\'20\'19s life was endangered. Eventually, the US ninth district court of appeals struck down the law, citing Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are very polarizing points of view within our small community on this emotionally charged issue,\u8221\'20\'1D said Williams.\par \par \uc2 Along with the vocal anti-abortionists, there has also been a local movement for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights led by CHamoru women, the Indigenous people of the Mariana Islands, which include Guam. The island was colonized by Spain in the 17th century, when Catholicism was introduced, and was ceded to the US in 1899.\par \par \uc2 Dr Michael Lujan Bevacqua, a CHamoru scholar and the son of Rita Lujan Bevacqua, a nurse who fought for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the 1990s, says \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights align with ancient CHamoru values and that CHamoru women were empowered to make choices for themselves.\par \par \uc2 TelAbortion, a pilot project in Hawai?i and 12 other states, allows certified doctors to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  consultations via videochat and mail the medications directly to patients. But two ambiguous laws in Guam, one of which is the 1978 law, have made medical professionals unsure how to proceed, prompting the \uc2 ACLU\uc2  and two Hawai?i doctors who are licensed to practice in Guam to file the recent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/feb/22/guam-abortions-aclu-lawsuit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lawsuit}}}\uc2  in January.\par \par \uc2 On 5 March the \uc2 ACLU\uc2  reached an agreement with Guam\u8217\'20\'19s attorney general and Board of Medical Examiners that means the 1978 will not be enforced. While that amounts to a major win in the case, Alexa Kolbi-Molinas, a senior staff attorney at the ACLU Reproductive Freedom Project, says that in practice \u8220\'20\'1Cthings will not change overnight\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 A key challenge still remains in overturning the second law, which requires patients to attend an in-person visit with a physician on the island at least 24 hours before an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to share mandated information. The \uc2 ACLU\uc2  says this creates undue logistical hurdles as well as privacy concerns. It also puts a physician in a tenuous situation, since punishment for violating the law includes loss of a medical license.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe settlement clears any legal obstacles to using telemedicine to prescribe medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kolbi-Molinas. \u8220\'20\'1CIt is still an open question in the case whether patients will be required to visit a different healthcare provider in Guam to obtain the mandated information.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 ACLU\uc2  will challenge the second law on 18 March in Guam\u8217\'20\'19s district court.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210310eh3a0010b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
