\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Biden accuses Republicans of 'Neanderthal thinking' for lifting mask mandates \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Maanvi Singh (now), Joan E Greve and Martin Belam (earlier) \par \uc2 14,105 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 March 2021\par 01:09 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 President criticizes Republican governors who rescinded mask mandatesCuomo says he will not quit over sexual harassment allegationsHouse leaders change voting schedule due to potential violenceBiden backs narrower eligibility for stimulus checksSign up to receive First Thing \u8211\'20\'13 our daily briefing by email\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.56am GMT\par \par \uc2 Summary\par \par \uc2 From Joan E Greve and me:\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  sharply criticized the Republican governors of Texas and Mississippi for rescinding their mask mandates despite public health experts\u8217\'20\'19 concerns about another potential surge in coronavirus cases. \u8220\'20\'1CI think it\u8217\'20\'19s a big mistake,\u8221\'20\'1D the president said of the governors\u8217\'20\'19 decisions. Noting that the US will have enough vaccines for all American adults by the end of May, Biden said: \u8220\'20\'1CThe last thing we need is Neanderthal thinking that in the meantime, everything\u8217\'20\'19s fine.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2  said he would not resign after three women accused him of sexual harassment. Speaking at a press conference today, the New York governor offered qualified apologies for his behavior and said he would \u8220\'20\'1Cfully cooperate\u8221\'20\'1D with the state attorney general\u8217\'20\'19s investigation of the allegations.\par \par \uc2 * House leaders changed their voting schedule for the week, amid threats of violence against the Capitol tomorrow. The House will now vote on Democrats \u8217\'20\'19 police reform bill tonight, rather than tomorrow. The schedule change came hours after the US \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  released a statement saying they had \u8220\'20\'1Cobtained intelligence that shows a possible plot to breach the Capitol by an identified militia group on Thursday, March 4\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 * The commanding general of the DC national guard said the Pentagon curtailed his ability to rapidly deploy guard troops the day before the 6 January insurrection. The national guard leader, William J Walker, said at a Senate hearing today that it took three hours to receive Pentagon approval to deploy guard troops to the Capitol after the building was breached by insurrectionists.\par \par \uc2 * Biden has agreed to phase out the direct payments in the coronavirus relief bill at a more rapid rate, according to multiple reports. Moderate Democrats like Senator Joe Manchin have proposed completely phasing out the checks for individuals who make $80,000 a year, rather than $100,000 a year, which was the threshold for the first two rounds of stimulus payments.\par \par \uc2 * The \uc2 Congressional Black Caucus (CBC) \uc2 has endorsed Shalanda Young to become the new nominee for \uc2 Office of Management and Budget (OMB) \uc2 director after Neera Tanden withdrew from consideration. Young is currently set to become deputy director of the OMB, and at her confirmation hearing for the deputy job received support from some Republicans for the top spot as well.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.09am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.31am GMT\par \par \uc2 The misogynist incel movement is spreading. Should it be classified as a terror threat?\par \par \uc2 As attacks linked to the misogynist \u8220\'20\'1Cincel\u8221\'20\'1D movement mounted in recent years, authorities around the world have begun to treat the ideology as a more serious terrorism threat.\par \par \uc2 Since 2014, men who call themselves \u8220\'20\'1Cinvoluntary celibates\u8221\'20\'1D and blame women for their own lack of sexual and social status have carried out mass killings in California, Florida, and Toronto.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, a judge in Canada delivered a guilty verdict for the perpetrator of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/toronto-van-incident"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2018 Toronto van attack}}}\uc2 , which left 10 people dead and 16 injured \u8211\'20\'13 the deadliest incident linked to the incel movement..\par \par \uc2 In her verdict, the judge found that the attacker\u8217\'20\'19s resentment towards women was one among several motivating factors, but experts warn that such incidents are becoming more frequent, and that the ideology behind them is spreading internationally.\par \par \uc2 2020 saw a string of incel-related incidents, including {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2020/may/19/toronto-attack-incel-terrorism-canada-police"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a machete attack at a Toronto massage parlor}}}\uc2 , a shooting at an Arizona mall which injured three people, a man who blew up his own hand with explosives in Virginia, and a New York man charged by federal prosecutors for targeting a couple with violent threats.\par \par \uc2 While only one of those attacks was fatal, \u8220\'20\'1Cthe fact those incidents weren\u8217\'20\'19t worse is more luck than good counterterrorism,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jacob Ware, a terrorism researcher who studies the incel movement. \u8220\'20\'1C2020 was a bad year for incel violence.\par \par \uc2 Read more:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/2021/mar/03/incel-movement-terror-threat-canada"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The misogynist incel movement is spreading. Should it be classified as a terror threat?}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.04am GMT\par \par \uc2 The transportation department\u8217\'20\'19s internal watchdog asked the justice department in December to consider a criminal investigation into \uc2 Elaine Chao\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s conduct as transportation secretary.\par \par \uc2 There was evidence that Chao used her position to promote her family\u8217\'20\'19s shipping business, the inspector general said in a report released today. In the final weeks of the Trump administration, the justice department declined to take up the issue.\par \par \uc2 There were several \u8220\'20\'1Cpotential ethics concerns arising from the actions of the Secretary and Office of the Secretary (OST) staff under her direction\u8221\'20\'1D, investigators said.\par \par \uc2 Among the violations was tasking political appointees to \u8220\'20\'1Ccontact the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  (DHS) about the status of a work permit application submitted by a foreign student studying at a US university who was a recipient of Chao family philanthropy\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Chao also planned a trip that she didn\u8217\'20\'19t end up taking in 2017 that included her father and sister, who runs the family shipping business. Chao, who is married to the Republican Senate minority leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , quit the job on 7 January, after the Capitol attack.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.34am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.30pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The House is now debating the George Floyd Justice in Policing Act\par \par \uc2 The Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 police reform bill passed through Congress last year, but was never taken up by the Republican-led Senate. This time around, the act has the support of the White House, but it is still unlikely to make it through the Senate. Although Democrats have control of the Senate, they don\u8217\'20\'19t have the 60 votes needed to overcome the filibuster and pass the legislation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are still trying to transform policing in the United States,\u8221\'20\'1D said Representative \uc2 Karen Bass\uc2  of California. She said she\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cconfident that we will be able to have a bipartisan bill in the Senate that will reach President Biden\u8217\'20\'19s desk.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.30pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.56pm GMT\par \par \uc2 San Francisco will begin reopening more of its economy amid declining Covid-19 case rates, hospitalizations and deaths.\par \par \uc2 Starting on Wednesday, restaurants can start serving limited indoor dining and movie theaters, gyms and museums can reopen at restricted capacity.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is the beginning of a great time in San Francisco, you save money not buying those plane tickets to go other places. You can enjoy your city, right here right now,\u8221\'20\'1D an upbeat Mayor London Breed said on Tuesday under blue skies from Pier 39, an area popular with tourists in picturesque Fisherman\u8217\'20\'19s Wharf.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI am so proud of San Francisco. Nearly a year after our shelter-in-place order, thanks to our collective actions and commitment to following the health guidelines, we have come through our worst surge since the beginning of the pandemic,\u8221\'20\'1D Dr Grant Colfax, the director of the San Francisco department of public health, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 San Francisco, with a population of 900,000 before the pandemic, has seen among the lowest number of coronavirus cases and death rates in the country, reporting more than 34,000 cases since the start of the pandemic and 422 deaths.\par \par \uc2 The city and its surrounding area were the first to impose a lockdown when the coronavirus struck the US in the spring of last year. Some sectors were allowed to reopen after case rates dropped in the summer, but the city shut down business activity again in early December as the positivity rate surged statewide.\par \par \uc2 City fiscal analysts say San Franciscans have stayed at home more than people in other {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/california"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 California}}}\uc2  cities and even other equally strict Bay Area counties, with many of the region\u8217\'20\'19s workers, including those in the tech industry, able to shift their work from offices to their homes. The policies contributed to good public health, but stressed the local economy.\par \par \uc2 Read more:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/03/san-francisco-reopen-restaurants-gyms-covid-19-cases"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 San Francisco to reopen indoor dining, gyms and museums as Covid cases fall}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.29pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Biden thanks House Democrats for their partnership at virtual meeting\par \par \uc2 After an introduction from \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , Biden thanked her and other congressional Democrats. \u8220\'20\'1CNancy, I love you,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CCongressman Clyburn, I also want to note that was almost exactly a year ago today that you delivered the endorsement to me in South Carolina that meant so much to me in my campaign, you\u8217\'20\'19re a great friend,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s endorsement was pivotal in turning the Democratic primaries in Biden\u8217\'20\'19s favor last year.\par \par \uc2 But Biden noted that so much has changed since he was a candidate.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAmerica confirmed fewer than 100 Covid cases,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CJust a year ago, Breonna Taylor and George Floyd were still alive. California had yet to suffer what would become the worst fire season in recorded history. And there\u8217\'20\'19d been no violent insurrection on Capitol Hill.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.27pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.20pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Congressional Black Caucus (CBC) \uc2 has endorsed Shalanda Young to become the new nominee for \uc2 Office of Management and Budget (OMB) \uc2 director after Neera Tanden withdrew from consideration.\par \par \uc2 Young is currently set to become deputy director of the OMB, and at her confirmation hearing for the deputy, job received support from some Republicans for the top spot as well.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou\u8217\'20\'19ll get my support, maybe for both jobs,\u8221\'20\'1D Republican senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  of South Carolina told Young yesterday.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is our understanding that Shalanda Young has been thoroughly vetted for the position of Deputy Director and has received excellent reviews from both Democratic and Republican members of the Senate,\u8221\'20\'1D CBC Chair \uc2 Joyce Beatty\uc2  of Ohio, along with eight other members, wrote in the letter to Biden.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CShalanda Young is uniquely qualified to lead OMB and assume the responsibility for developing the President\u8217\'20\'19s budget and supervising the administration of executive branch agencies.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Tanden withdrew yesterday, facing slim chances of winning enough Senate support to be confirmed as director.\par \par \uc2 Read more:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/03/neera-tanden-analysis-biden-cabinet-nomination"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Scramble on to replace Neera Tanden after nomination met perfect storm}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.00pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Today so far\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s it from me today. My west coast colleague Maanvi Singh will take over the blog for the next few hours.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s where the day stands so far:\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  sharply criticized the Republican governors of Texas and Mississippi for rescinding their mask mandates, despite public health experts\u8217\'20\'19 concerns about another potential surge in coronavirus cases. \u8220\'20\'1CI think it\u8217\'20\'19s a big mistake,\u8221\'20\'1D the president said of the governors\u8217\'20\'19 decisions. Noting that the US will have enough vaccines for all American adults by the end of May, Biden said: \u8220\'20\'1CThe last thing we need is Neanderthal thinking that in the meantime, everything\u8217\'20\'19s fine.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2  said he would not resign after three women accused him of sexual harassment. Speaking at a press conference today, the New York governor offered qualified apologies for his behavior and said he would \u8220\'20\'1Cfully cooperate\u8221\'20\'1D with the state attorney general\u8217\'20\'19s investigation of the allegations.\par \par \uc2 * House leaders changed their voting schedule for the week, amid threats of violence against the Capitol tomorrow. The House will now vote on Democrats \u8217\'20\'19 police reform bill tonight, rather than tomorrow. The schedule change came hours after the US \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  released a statement saying it had \u8220\'20\'1Cobtained intelligence that shows a possible plot to breach the Capitol by an identified militia group on Thursday, March 4\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 * The commanding general of the DC national guard said the Pentagon curtailed his ability to rapidly deploy guard troops the day before the 6 January insurrection. The national guard leader, William J Walker, said at a Senate hearing today that it took three hours to receive Pentagon approval to deploy guard troops to the Capitol after the building was breached by insurrectionists.\par \par \uc2 * Biden has agreed to phase out the direct payments in the coronavirus relief bill at a more rapid rate, according to multiple reports. Moderate Democrats like Senator Joe Manchin have proposed completely phasing out the checks for individuals who make $80,000 a year, rather than $100,000 a year, which was the threshold for the first two rounds of stimulus payments.\par \par \uc2 Maanvi will have more coming up, so stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.38pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.37pm GMT\par \par \uc2 House leaders change voting schedule after threats of violence\par \par \uc2 The House majority leader, \uc2 Steny Hoyer\uc2 , confirmed the chamber will vote on Democrats \u8217\'20\'19 police reform bill tonight, instead of tomorrow, after the US \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  (USCP) warned of a militia\u8217\'20\'19s potential plot to storm the Capitol tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 According to Hoyer\u8217\'20\'19s office, the House will reconvene at 6.15pm ET for one hour of debate on the George Floyd Justice in Policing Act.\par \par \uc2 The chamber will then vote on that proposal, as well as Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 election reform bill, the For the People Act.\par \par \uc2 The schedule change comes hours after the USCP released a statement saying it had \u8220\'20\'1Cobtained intelligence that shows a possible plot to breach the Capitol by an identified militia group on Thursday, March 4\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have already made significant security upgrades to include establishing a physical structure and increasing manpower to ensure the protection of Congress, the public and our police officers,\u8221\'20\'1D the USCP said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur department is working with our local, state, and federal partners to stop any threats to the Capitol. We are taking the intelligence seriously.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.42pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.16pm GMT\par \par \uc2 House leaders are reportedly discussing the possibility of wrapping up their voting for the week tonight, so members do not have to return to Capitol Hill tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 If House leadership chose to move forward with that schedule, members would have to vote on Democrats \u8217\'20\'19 police reform bill tonight.\par \par \uc2 enltrThe House is DEBATING whether to stay tonight to finish voting for the week instead of coming back in tomorrow in light of Capitol Police warning about security risks for Thursday, a Democratic aide tells me. This means House would vote on police reform tn. Not decided yet tho.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Annie Grayer (@AnnieGrayerCNN) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/AnnieGrayerCNN/status/1367214352806383620?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 3, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 The discussions come hours after the US \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  released a statement saying it is aware of a militia\u8217\'20\'19s potential attempts to storm the Capitol tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have obtained intelligence that shows a possible plot to breach the Capitol by an identified militia group on Thursday, March 4,\u8221\'20\'1D the USCP said in a statement this morning.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have already made significant security upgrades to include establishing a physical structure and increasing manpower to ensure the protection of Congress, the public and our police officers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Far-right conspiracy theorists have said 4 March is the true inauguration date, when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will be sworn in as the rightful president.\par \par \uc2 That is of course not at all true. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  fairly won the presidential election and was sworn in on 20 January.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.43pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.58pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BBC\uc2  has issued a correction and apology on its website for airing an interview with a man who claimed to be the US senator {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/cory-booker"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Cory Booker}}}\uc2  but was actually someone impersonating the Democratic politician from New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 The UK\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 public broadcasting service\uc2  said that the interview appeared \u8220\'20\'1Cto be a deliberate hoax\u8221\'20\'1D and that it had reached out with an apology to Booker and \u8220\'20\'1Care looking into what went wrong to make sure it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t happen again\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The interview was aired on 26 February on the \uc2 BBC\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Newshour radio program. The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/bbc"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 BBC}}}\uc2  said that the interview \u8220\'20\'1Chas not appeared elsewhere\u8221\'20\'1D, and no audio clips or videos are circulating online, though one \uc2 Twitter\uc2  user heard the interview on New York\u8217\'20\'19s WNYC public radio station and could tell something was amiss.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CListening to the @bbcworldservice Newshour on @wnyc and trying to figure out how they did an entire interview with someone they introduced as Senator \uc2 Cory Booker\uc2 , who I\u8217\'20\'19m pretty sure was definitely not Senator Booker, and didn\u8217\'20\'19t realize it,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/AEasonNY/status/1365400229139402760?s=20"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote}}}\uc2 Twitter\uc2  user Amy Eason.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2021/mar/03/bbc-cory-booker-impostor-hoax-interview"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 BBC apologizes for interview with man impersonating US senator Cory Booker}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.40pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Republican senators intend to force Senate clerks to read the full text of the coronavirus relief bill aloud, according to CNN.\par \par \uc2 That move alone could delay the beginning of the Senate\u8217\'20\'19s vote-a-rama on the relief bill by up to 10 hours.\par \par \uc2 enltrRepublicans are telling me they plan to force Senate clerks to read the full $1.9 trillion relief bill aloud. Typically that process is dispensed with, but any single member can object and force a full reading of the bill.\par \par \uc2 That alone could take 10 hours \u8212\'20\'14 even before votes begin\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Manu Raju (@mkraju) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/mkraju/status/1367193921542045698?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 3, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 Once the Senate moves on to the vote-a-rama, Republicans intend to introduce many amendments in order to delay the final vote on the legislation.\par \par \uc2 Republicans have said they will use the amendments to highlight what they view as the most controversial aspects of the relief bill, which they have attacked as a progressive wish list.\par \par \uc2 Despite Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 delay tactics, the bill is expected to eventually pass the Senate in a mostly (if not entirely) party-line vote.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.43pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.22pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Daniel Strauss reports:\par \par \uc2 Neera Tanden \u8217\'20\'19s decision to withdraw from consideration to serve as \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s budget director marks the first major loss for the still young {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/biden-administration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Biden administration}}}\uc2 , and sets off a scramble between various political factions to push through a new nominee.\par \par \uc2 Tanden was the first of Biden\u8217\'20\'19s cabinet nominees to fail to make it through the confirmation process. New presidents don\u8217\'20\'19t usually see all of their cabinet picks confirmed.\par \par \uc2 But Tanden\u8217\'20\'19s path was always more precarious than the rest. She is well known throughout the Democratic party as a combative figure who often engaged in \uc2 Twitter\uc2  fights and criticized both Republicans and Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Republicans at moments seemed to revel in highlighting Tanden\u8217\'20\'19s past tweets. But those senators also expressed eagerness to support one of the potential replacements \u8211\'20\'13 Shalanda Young, a veteran Hill staffer who is Black.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou know I\u8217\'20\'19m going to vote for you,\u8221\'20\'1D Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  of South Carolina, the ranking Republican on the Senate budget committee, said on Tuesday during Young\u8217\'20\'19s hearing to serve as deputy director of the Office of Management and Budget. \u8220\'20\'1CYou\u8217\'20\'19re highly qualified and I\u8217\'20\'19m going to support you,\u8221\'20\'1D Graham added before pressing her on immigration policy.\par \par \uc2 Other names have been floated as possible OMB nominees and the Senate budget committee is now waiting for the White House to pick someone else. The names floated include Sarah Bianchi, a former director of policy for Biden; Gene Sperling, a former director of the council of economic advisers; Ann O\u8217\'20\'19Leary, the former chief of staff to Governor Gavin Newsom of California.\par \par \uc2 Whoever the White House nominates is poised to have an easier confirmation process than Tanden.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/03/neera-tanden-analysis-biden-cabinet-nomination"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Scramble on to replace Neera Tanden after nomination met perfect storm}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.02pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Biden accuses Republican governors of 'Neanderthal thinking' after rescinding mask mandates\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  sharply criticized the Republican governors of Texas and Mississippi, who announced yesterday that they are rescinding their mask mandates, despite public health experts\u8217\'20\'19 concerns about another surge in coronavirus cases.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think it\u8217\'20\'19s a big mistake,\u8221\'20\'1D the president said of the governors\u8217\'20\'19 announcements.\par \par \uc2 enltrNEW: President Biden calls Texas and Mississippi decisions to end mask mandates \u8220\'20\'1Ca big mistake" and criticizes what he views as \u8220\'20\'1CNeanderthal thinking\u8221\'20\'1D after CDC warned against complacency in the face of emerging coronavirus variants on Monday. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/Mmdln3gNG6"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/Mmdln3gNG6}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 NBC News (@NBCNews) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/NBCNews/status/1367192228762963971?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 3, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 Biden emphasized that scientific evidence shows masks make a crucial difference in limiting the spread of coronavirus.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are on the cusp of being able to fundamentally change the nature of this disease because of the way in which we are able to get vaccines in people\u8217\'20\'19s arms,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden said.\par \par \uc2 The president reiterated his announcement yesterday that the US will have enough coronavirus vaccines for all American adults by the end of May.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe last thing we need is Neanderthal thinking that in the meantime, everything\u8217\'20\'19s fine,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt still matters.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Dr Rochelle Walensky\uc2 , the director of the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, similarly voiced concern about the governors\u8217\'20\'19 decision during the White House coronavirus response team\u8217\'20\'19s briefing today.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe at the CDC have been very clear that now is not the time to release all restrictions,\u8221\'20\'1D Walensky said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe next month or two is really pivotal in terms of how this pandemic goes.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.48pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Early afternoon summary\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s been a very lively day so far in US political news and there will be a lot more action in the coming hours, so stay tuned. Here are the main events so far today:\par \par \uc2 * New York governorAndrew Cuomo, who became very well known beyond the state for his daily, plain-spoken briefings during the first coronavirus surge, a short while ago {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/05/us-immigration-biden-remain-in-mexico-asylum-seekers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 faced the press again}}}\uc2  and gave qualified apologies for behavior that some women have complained amounted to sexual harassment, but said he would not resign.\par \par \uc2 * Cuomo said he will {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2021/mar/03/joe-biden-covid-coronavirus-vaccine-xavier-becerra-donald-trump-live-updates?page=with:block-603fd57b8f08be9d080da1f9#block-603fd57b8f08be9d080da1f9"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fully cooperate}}}\uc2  with the investigation that has been started into his conduct by the New York state attorney general, Letitia James.\par \par \uc2 * The leader of the Washington, DC, National Guard, William Walker, told a Senate panel that the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2021/mar/03/joe-biden-covid-coronavirus-vaccine-xavier-becerra-donald-trump-live-updates?page=with:block-603fc1218f08d196fd22d343#block-603fc1218f08d196fd22d343"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Pentagon restricted}}}\uc2  his ability to call out troops to back up the police at the US Capitol during the insurrection on January 6.\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has reportedly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2021/mar/03/joe-biden-covid-coronavirus-vaccine-xavier-becerra-donald-trump-live-updates?page=with:block-603fb1e78f08be9d080d9f11#block-603fb1e78f08be9d080d9f11"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 agreed to phase out}}}\uc2  the $1,400 direct payments in the coronavirus relief package at a more rapid rate then expected.\par \par \uc2 * The \uc2 US Capitol Police\uc2  says it has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2021/mar/03/joe-biden-covid-coronavirus-vaccine-xavier-becerra-donald-trump-live-updates?page=with:block-603faadf8f08d196fd22d172#block-603faadf8f08d196fd22d172"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 obtained intelligence}}}\uc2  about a militia group potentially attempting to attack the US Capitol tomorrow. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  issued a similar warning around midnight last night, though the House acting sergeant at arms said online extremist chatter around this has quietened down of late.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.49pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.13pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Cuomo said there were no buts about his qualified apology, but there were plenty of \u8220\'20\'1Cifs\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The governor got off on a terrible foot by saying he was sorry \u8220\'20\'1Cif anyone was offended\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 it\u8217\'20\'19s very clear from the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/01/andrew-cuomo-new-york-governor-apology-sexual-harassment-resign-calls-"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 public accounts}}}\uc2  so far from three women who accuse Cuomo of sliming around them that they were offended.\par \par \uc2 He also said he had never touched anyone inappropriately. Which is his take on that word, when it\u8217\'20\'19s very clear from the most recent photographs, at least, that he has touched someone a fraction of his age, with little of his power, inappropriately.\par \par \uc2 When asked if he had been given the standard anti-sexual harassment training that his public employee colleagues receive, he said, in a word, \u8220\'20\'1C yes \u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 And Cuomo said: \u8220\'20\'1C I never knew at the time that I was making anyone uncomfortable. \u8221\'20\'1D Which, in a no-win situation, sounds better than I knew/had a pretty good idea, but I just behaved like a power oaf and bullied onwards.\par \par \uc2 And it\u8217\'20\'19s interesting that the second woman publicly to accuse Cuomo, Charlotte Bennett, submitted detailed complaints to senior staff at the New York seat of government in Albany, upstate, about the governor\u8217\'20\'19s conduct.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/01/andrew-cuomo-new-york-governor-apology-sexual-harassment-resign-calls-"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Cuomo faces new calls to resign as harassment investigation looms}}}\par \par \uc2 The New York attorney general, Letitia James, is investigating the allegations against Cuomo.\par \par \uc2 Letitia James at an Asian American Foundation \u8216\'20\'18Rise Up Against Asian Hate\u8217\'20\'19 rally in New York on 27 February. Photograph: Sachyn Mital/REX/\uc2 Shutterstock\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.27pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.50pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Asked what his message is to New Yorkers, \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2  said he was \u8220\'20\'1Cembarrassed\u8221\'20\'1D that his behavior had such a negative impact on one of his aides. He did not appear to address the allegations from two other women.\par \par \uc2 The governor said he was not caveating his apology in any way. \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s no \u8216\'20\'18buts.\u8217\'20\'19 I\u8217\'20\'19m sorry,\u8221\'20\'1D Cuomo said.\par \par \uc2 But the governor did attempt to qualify his apology multiple times by emphasizing he did not intend to hurt or offend anyone through his actions.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.45pm GMT\par \par \uc2 'I\u8217\'20\'19m not going to resign,' Cuomo says after being accused of sexual misconduct\par \par \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2  made clear that he would not resign in the wake of multiple sexual misconduct allegations against him.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m not going to resign,\u8221\'20\'1D the New York governor said at his press conference. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m going to do the job the people of the state elected me to do.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A number of lawmakers of both parties have called on the Democratic governor to resign as the state attorney general investigates the allegations against him.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI do not believe I have ever done anything in my public career that I am ashamed of,\u8221\'20\'1D Cuomo said.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.28pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.41pm GMT\par \par \uc2 New York Governor \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2  said that he has learned from the experience of having multiple women accuse him of sexual misconduct.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m sorry,\u8221\'20\'1D Cuomo said. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m sorry for whatever pain I caused anyone. I never intended it, and I will be the better for this experience.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Cuomo acknowledged that his intentions did not matter when his behavior had such a negative impact on the women affected by it. And yet, the governor continued to emphasize that he did not intend to hurt or offend anyone.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf they were offended by it, it was wrong,\u8221\'20\'1D Cuomo said. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd if they were offended by it, I apologize.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.38pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Cuomo says he will 'fully cooperate' with attorney general's investigation\par \par \uc2 New York Governor \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2  said at a press conference moments ago that he will \u8220\'20\'1Cfully cooperate\u8221\'20\'1D with the state attorney general\u8217\'20\'19s investigation of sexual misconduct allegations against him.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI fully support a woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to come forward, and I think it should be encouraged in every way,\u8221\'20\'1D Cuomo said.\par \par \uc2 The Democratic went on to apologize for engaging in behavior that made anyone feel uncomfortable.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI now understand that I acted in a way that made people feel uncomfortable,\u8221\'20\'1D Cuomo said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt was unintentional, and I truly and deeply apologize for it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But Cuomo went on to say, \u8220\'20\'1CI never touched anyone inappropriately.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The governor asked the people of New York to wait for the attorney general\u8217\'20\'19s investigation to conclude before reaching any conclusions about him.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI ask the people of this state to wait for the facts from the attorney general\u8217\'20\'19s report before forming an opinion. Get the facts please before forming an opinion,\u8221\'20\'1D Cuomo said. \u8220\'20\'1CI will fully cooperate with it, and then you will have the facts.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.21pm GMT\par \par \uc2 White House press secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  was asked when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will start naming his nominees for key ambassadorships.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a popular question, including from some people who want to be ambassadors, which won\u8217\'20\'19t surprise you,\u8221\'20\'1D Psaki told reporters.\par \par \uc2 The press secretary noted past administrations have often nominated ambassadors in March, but she was not sure whether Biden would follow that timeline, given his current focus on responding to the coronavirus pandemic.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.13pm GMT\par \par \uc2 White House press secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  dodged a question about whether \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is breaking his promise to millions of Americans who will no longer receive stimulus payments if the faster phaseout is authorized.\par \par \uc2 Psaki emphasized Biden was following through on delivering direct relief to American families, while sidestepping the fact that fewer families would now be receiving checks.\par \par \uc2 Asked whether there might be another round of direct payments in the future, Psaki said, \u8220\'20\'1CI can\u8217\'20\'19t predict for you there will never be stimulus checks in the future.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.09pm GMT\par \par \uc2 White House press secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  was asked why the Biden administration seems to be prioritizing teachers over other frontline workers.\par \par \uc2 A reporter noted some have suggested that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is bowing to political pressure from teachers\u8217\'20\'19 unions, who have expressed strong opposition to sending teachers back to the classroom before they are vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 Psaki said teachers are a vaccination priority because the reopening of schools has an impact on the next generation of American children and the future of the workforce.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.57pm GMT\par \par \uc2 White House press secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  criticized the Republican governors of Texas and Mississippi for rescinding their mask mandates as health experts warn of another potential surge in coronavirus cases.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis entire country has paid the price for political leaders who ignored the science,\u8221\'20\'1D Psaki said.\par \par \uc2 The press secretary said \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  would raise the issue the next time he speaks with governors. She encouraged Americans to follow the guidance of public health experts, who are \u8220\'20\'1Cbasing their recommendations on how to save people\u8217\'20\'19s lives\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Dr Rochelle Walensky\uc2 , the director of the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, said earlier today, \u8220\'20\'1CEvery individual is empowered to do the right thing here, regardless of what the states decide.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.53pm GMT\par \par \uc2 White House press secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  would not definitively say whether \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has signed off on more rapidly phasing out the $1,400 checks in the coronavirus relief bill.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHe is comfortable with where the negotiations stand,\u8221\'20\'1D Psaki said. She added, \u8220\'20\'1CWe don\u8217\'20\'19t have a final bill.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The press secretary emphasized there were \u8220\'20\'1Congoing discussions\u8221\'20\'1D about the specifics of the bill, and the White House expected there to be \u8220\'20\'1Ctweaks on the margins\u8221\'20\'1D as the Senate moves toward final passage.\par \par \uc2 Reports have indicated Biden has signaled support for completely phasing out the checks for individuals who make $80,000 a year, rather than $100,000 a year.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.46pm GMT\par \par \uc2 White House press secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  said \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  does not intend to name another nominee to lead the Office of Management and Budget this week.\par \par \uc2 Psaki told reporters that she would not get into the White House\u8217\'20\'19s discussions with specific senators regarding Neera Tanden\u8217\'20\'19s nomination.\par \par \uc2 Tanden withdrew her nomination last night, saying she no longer believed she had a path to confirmation.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.44pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s press secretary, \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , is now holding her daily briefing with reporters at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Psaki announced the Biden administration would release its \u8220\'20\'1Cinterim national security strategic guidance\u8221\'20\'1D on the White House website this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 The guidance will provide recommendations to government departments and agencies, before the administration releases its full national security strategy later this year.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.24pm GMT\par \par \uc2 First lady Jill Biden has arrived in Connecticut, where she is visiting an elementary school with Education Secretary Miguel Cardona.\par \par \uc2 Biden and Cardona, both former educators, are visiting Benjamin Franklin Elementary School in Meriden, Connecticut, and they will later travel to Fort LeBoeuf Middle School in Waterford, Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 enltr.? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/FLOTUS?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @FLOTUS}}}\uc2  ? and Education Secretary Miguel Cardona greeting students in a classroom during visit to Benjamin Franklin Elementary School in Meriden, Conn. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/izsUA0QjxS"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/izsUA0QjxS}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 darlene superville (@dsupervilleap) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/dsupervilleap/status/1367159307167428608?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 3, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 The trip comes two days after the Senate confirmed Cardona, in a vote of 64 to 33. Cardona has said that safely reopening schools will be his top priority as education secretary.\par \par \uc2 Previewing the trip yesterday, White House press secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  said, \u8220\'20\'1CThey will highlight the key CDC mitigation strategies that the schools have implemented successfully in these locations; listen to the challenges they are facing due to the pandemic, including the academic, social, and emotional needs of students; highlight the additional resources in the American Rescue Plan needed for schools to open -- remain open; and address the needs of students, and thank educators for their work in supporting students and their families.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.09pm GMT\par \par \uc2 DC national guard leader says Pentagon restricted his ability to deploy troops\par \par \uc2 Over at the Senate hearing on the Capitol insurrection, William J Walker, the commanding general of the DC national guard, said he was required to receive Pentagon approval before deploying Guard troops on 6 January.\par \par \uc2 Walker noted that such an approval process was not put in place for the summer protests in DC, following the police killing of George Floyd. During those protests, Walker was able to quickly deploy guard troops himself.\par \par \uc2 The general said his ability to deploy a quick-reaction force was restricted by acting defense secretary \uc2 Christopher Miller\uc2  on 5 January, the day before the insurrection.\par \par \uc2 Maj Gen William J Walker testifies before two Senate committees. Photograph: Shawn Thew/REX/\uc2 Shutterstock\par \par \uc2 Walker said that, if he had the ability to deploy a quick-reaction force, \u8220\'20\'1CI would have sent them there immediately as soon as I hung up\u8221\'20\'1D with US Capitol police chief Steven Sund.\par \par \uc2 Walker added he was \u8220\'20\'1Cstunned\u8221\'20\'1D by the laborious approval process for troop deployment because he had never seen anything like it in his 19 years of service.\par \par \uc2 The commanding general previously testified that it took more than three hours to receive approval from the Pentagon to deploy guard troops on 6 January.\par \par \uc2 If Walker had the ability to deploy a quick-reaction force, he could have sent 150 troops to the Capitol in 20 minutes, he told senators.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.27pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.53pm GMT\par \par \uc2 House Democratic leaders are encouraging \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to choose Shalanda Young to replace Neera Tanden as his nominee to lead the Office of Management and Budget.\par \par \uc2 The statement from House speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , majority leader \uc2 Steny Hoyer\uc2  and majority whip \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2  comes a day after Tanden withdrew her nomination as OMB director due to bipartisan opposition to her confirmation.\par \par \uc2 Shalanda Young testifies in a confirmation hearing before the \uc2 Senate Committee on the Budget\uc2 . Photograph: REX/\uc2 Shutterstock\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNeera Tanden is an outstanding public servant who has dedicated decades to fighting to advance the health, financial security and well-being of the American people. We know that she will continue to bring her progressive values, bold vision for the future and valuable perspective to Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 work to Build Back Better,\u8221\'20\'1D the three House leaders said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs longtime Members of the Appropriations Committee, we take great pride in recommending Shalanda Young as Director of the Office of Management and Budget. We have worked closely with her for several years and highly recommend her for her intellect, her deep expertise on the federal budget and her determination to ensure that our budget reflects our values as a nation.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Democratic leaders added that Young\u8217\'20\'19s nomination would be \u8220\'20\'1Chistoric,\u8221\'20\'1D as she would be the first African American woman to lead OMB if she were confirmed.\par \par \uc2 Young, a longtime Capitol Hill staffer, has already been nominated for the deputy OMB director post, and that nomination has attracted praise from senators of both parties.\par \par \uc2 During her confirmation hearing yesterday, Republican Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  explicitly told Young that he would be happy to support her if she were nominated for the top job at OMB.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.34pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Dr Rochelle Walensky\uc2 , the director of the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, noted that Americans can still follow best practices to limit their risk of contracting coronavirus, even if their states are lifting restrictions.\par \par \uc2 enltrCDC Director \uc2 Rochelle Walensky\uc2  responds to \uc2 Texas Gov. Greg Abbott\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1C100%\u8221\'20\'1D reopening:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEvery individual is empowered to do the right thing here, regardless of what the states decide \u8230\'20\'26\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/ozALoVI2u5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/ozALoVI2u5}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 The Recount (@therecount) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/therecount/status/1367149972978868225?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 3, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEvery individual is empowered to do the right thing here, regardless of what the states decide -- for personal health, for public health, for the health of their loved ones and communities,\u8221\'20\'1D Walensky said.\par \par \uc2 She added, \u8220\'20\'1CI would still encourage individuals to wear a mask, to socially distance and to do the right thing to protect their own health.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.28pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The director of the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention expressed disapproval of states lifting some of their coronavirus-related public health orders.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve been very clear that now is not the time to release all restrictions. The next month or two is really pivotal,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Dr Rochelle Walensky\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 Walensky\u8217\'20\'19s comments come one day after the Republican governors of Texas and Mississippi announced they were lifting their mask mandates, despite health officials\u8217\'20\'19 concerns about another potential surge in cases.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.21pm GMT\par \par \uc2 CDC director encourages Americans to receive whichever vaccine is available\par \par \uc2 The White House coronavirus response team is now holding a briefing to provide an update on the vaccine distribution process.\par \par \uc2 Andy Slavitt\uc2 , a senior White House adviser, said health insurance companies are stepping up their efforts to get all seniors vaccinated, given that the elderly are more likely to become severely ill after contracting coronavirus.\par \par \uc2 Dr Rochelle Walensky\uc2 , the director of the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, also encouraged all Americans to \u8220\'20\'1Croll up your sleeves and get vaccinated with the first vaccine that\u8217\'20\'19s available to you.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The officials\u8217\'20\'19 comments come one day after \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  announced the US would have enough coronavirus vaccines for all American adults by the end of May.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.07pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Biden agrees to phase out stimulus checks more rapidly - reports\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has reportedly agreed to phase out the $1,400 direct payments in the coronavirus relief package at a more rapid rate.\par \par \uc2 Bloomberg News {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2021-03-03/biden-signs-off-on-tighter-eligibility-rules-for-stimulus-checks?sref=6flipp83"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has details}}}\uc2  on the changes:\par \par \uc2 Individuals earning over $80,000 now won\u8217\'20\'19t qualify for the payments, compared with a $100,000 cap in the previously drafted legislation, the aide said on condition of anonymity. The ceiling for couples will now be $160,000 against $200,000 before.\par \par \uc2 Democratic Senators including Joe Manchin of West Virginia and Jeanne Shaheen of New Hampshire had advocated tighter targeting of help to the neediest in the giant Covid-19 assistance package. Their votes will be critical in passing the legislation given the Senate\u8217\'20\'19s 50-50 partisan split and united Republican opposition to Biden\u8217\'20\'19s bill.\par \par \uc2 A separate push by moderates to trim the $400-a-week supplemental unemployment benefits in the bill that the House passed last week to $300 won\u8217\'20\'19t be included in what is initially brought to the Senate floor, according to the aide. The Senate\u8217\'20\'19s so-called managers\u8217\'20\'19 amendment to the House bill is expected to keep the House\u8217\'20\'19s figure, which is a $100-a-week increase from the current level through August.\par \par \uc2 The move will likely spark intense criticism among progressives, who had already expressed disappointment that the checks were not the full $2,000 amount that Biden campaigned on.\par \par \uc2 The White House has said the $1,400 checks, in combination with the $600 checks passed before Biden took office, collectively add up to $2,000 and thus the president is still keeping his promise.\par \par \uc2 But that will be harder to argue now that there is a segment of the American population who will no longer receive checks when they were previously expecting reduced payments.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.46pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US Capitol Police\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s new warning clashes with a statement from the House sergeant at arms yesterday, which said chatter among far-right extremists about March 4 as the true inauguration date had somewhat subsided.\par \par \uc2 enltrThe House Sergeant at Arms issues a bulletin saying that the significance of 3/4 as the "true" inauguration date for Trump among some groups has declined and they are no longer anticipating protests or violence from groups traveling to DC that day --> {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/NJBT26FDkk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/NJBT26FDkk}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Rebecca Kaplan (@RebeccaRKaplan) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/RebeccaRKaplan/status/1366767427967717378?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 2, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.39pm GMT\par \par \uc2 USCP is aware of a 'possible plot to breach the Capitol' tomorrow\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US Capitol Police\uc2  says it has obtained intelligence about a militia group potentially attempting to breach the Capitol tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have obtained intelligence that shows a possible plot to breach the Capitol by an identified militia group on Thursday, March 4. We have already made significant security upgrades to include establishing a physical structure and increasing manpower to ensure the protection of Congress, the public and our police officers,\u8221\'20\'1D the USCP said in a new statement.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur Department is working with our local, state, and federal partners to stop any threats to the Capitol. We are taking the intelligence seriously. Due to the sensitive nature of this information, we cannot provide additional details at this time.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Other law enforcement officials have expressed concerns about potential violence on March 4, which far-right extremists have identified as the date that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will be inaugurated as president.\par \par \uc2 The US Constitution previously mandated that presidents be inaugurated on March 4, but the 20th amendment pushed the presidential inauguration up to January 20.\par \par \uc2 Of course, Trump lost the presidential election, and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was sworn in as the rightful winner of the presidential race on January 20.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.24pm GMT\par \par \uc2 A senior Pentagon official offered a conflicting account of when the deployment of DC National Guard troops was approved on January 6.\par \par \uc2 Robert G. Salesses, the acting assistant secretary of defense for homeland defense and global security, confirmed that Capitol Police chief Steven Sund requested assistance at 1:49 pm on January 6.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFollowing a call with the Mayor of DC and her staff, the Secretary of the Army met with the Acting Secretary of Defense and the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff to discuss the requests of the \uc2 U.S. Capitol Police\uc2  and the Mayor of DC (at approximately 2:30 p.m.),\u8221\'20\'1D Salesses said in his prepared opening remarks.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Acting Secretary of Defense determined that all available forces of the DC National Guard were required to be re-missioned to reinforce DC MPD and U.S. Capitol Police positions to support efforts to reestablish security of the U.S. Capitol Complex. The Acting Secretary of Defense approved full activation of the DC National Guard to provide support, and the Secretary of the Army directed DC National Guard personnel to initiate movement and full mobilization (3:04 p.m.).\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Salesses went on to say, \u8220\'20\'1CAfter reviewing the DC National Guard forces\u8217\'20\'19 missions, equipping, and responsibilities to be performed at the Capitol Complex in support of DC MPD and U.S. Capitol Police, and conferring with the DC MPD at their headquarters at 4:10 p.m., the Secretary of the Army received the Acting Secretary of Defense\u8217\'20\'19s approval at 4:32 p.m., and ordered the DC National Guard forces to depart the Armory for the Capitol.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In contrast, William J. Walker, the commanding general of the DC National Guard, said the acting secretary of defense\u8217\'20\'19s approval was not relayed until 5:08 p.m., more than three hours after assistance was requested.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.08pm GMT\par \par \uc2 DC national guard leader describes three-hour delay in Army response to Capitol riot\par \par \uc2 The Senate homeland security committee and the Senate rules committee is now starting their second joint hearing on the security failures that occurred during the Capitol insurrection.\par \par \uc2 In his prepared opening remarks, William J. Walker, the commanding general of the DC National Guard, said it took army leaders more than three hours to approve a request for Guard troops to be deployed to the Capitol on 6 January.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAt 1:49pm I received a frantic call from then chief of US Capitol Police, Steven Sund, where he informed me that the security perimeter at the Capitol had been breached by hostile rioters. Chief Sund, his voice cracking with emotion, indicated that there was a dire emergency on Capitol Hill and requested the immediate assistance of as many guardsmen as I could muster,\u8221\'20\'1D Walker said in his prepared remarks.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CImmediately after the 1.49pm call with Chief Sund, I alerted the army senior leadership of the request. The approval for Chief Sund\u8217\'20\'19s request would eventually come from the acting secretary of defense and be relayed to me by army senior leaders at 5.08pm \u8211\'20\'13 three hours and 19 minutes later. We already had guardsmen on buses ready to move to the Capitol. Consequently, at 5.20pm (in under 20 minutes) the District of Columbia National Guard arrived at the Capitol. We helped to re-establish the security perimeter at the east side of the Capitol to facilitate the resumption of the joint session of Congress.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The delay is particularly alarming given that \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  missed clashing with insurrectionists by a matter of minutes, around 2.15pm on 6 January.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.16pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.50pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration \u8217\'20\'19s use of the video teleconferencing app Zoom to conduct its unclassified meetings has raised some security concerns in relation to China.\par \par \uc2 The Washington Post {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/opinions/2021/03/03/white-house-zoom-biden-meetings-china-cybersecurity/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reports}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 The Biden White House is using the teleconference platform Zoom for most of its unclassified government-related virtual interactions, even as the Justice Department is prosecuting one of the company\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/technology/2020/12/18/zoom-helped-china-surveillance/?itid=lk_inline_manual_2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 China-based executives}}}\uc2  for working with Beijing\u8217\'20\'19s intelligence services to interfere in Zoom calls. Some lawmakers, former officials and experts are warning that the Biden administration may be ignoring the risks.\par \par \uc2 In December, during the presidential transition, the DOJ {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/china-based-executive-us-telecommunications-company-charged-disrupting-video-meetings"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unveiled an indictment}}}\uc2  and issued an arrest warrant for Xinjiang Jin, also known as \u8216\'20\'18Juliean Jin,\u8217\'20\'19 a China-based Zoom employee, accusing him of manipulating Zoom\u8217\'20\'19s internal systems to interfere in U.S. calls that included content criticizing the Chinese Communist Party....\par \par \uc2 Two senior Biden administration officials told me that they inherited the Zoom system from the Trump administration, meaning they did not initiate the contract. They also emphasized the White House uses Zoom for Government, a more secure version, and not for classified meetings. All Zoom for Government communications \u8216\'20\'18 are processed exclusively in continental U.S. data centers that are managed solely by U.S.-based, U.S. people,\u8217\'20\'19 a Zoom spokesperson told me. The officials said the Biden administration would do a full review of all its communications policies and procedures but they declined to share any details of that review.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.31pm GMT\par \par \uc2 This is Joan Greve in Washington, taking over for Martin Belam.\par \par \uc2 The Senate homeland security committee and the Senate rules committee will soon hold their second joint hearing on the security failures that occurred during the Capitol insurrection on 6 January.\par \par \uc2 Last week, the former chief of the \uc2 US Capitol Police\uc2  largely blamed the security failures on a lack of intelligence about the likelihood for violence at the 6 January pro-Trump march in Washington.\par \par \uc2 FBI Director \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  testifies during a hearing before the Senate judiciary committee. Photograph: Xinhua/REX/\uc2 Shutterstock\par \par \uc2 But testifying before the Senate judiciary committee yesterday \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2 , the FBI director, said members of the \uc2 US Capitol Police\uc2  and the Metropolitan Police Department were made aware of a 5 January field office report, which warned of potential violence at the Capitol the next day.\par \par \uc2 Wray\u8217\'20\'19s testimony only intensified questions over why DC law enforcement leaders did not adequately prepare for an attack that many saw coming.\par \par \uc2 The hearing will begin in about half an hour, so stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.38pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.20pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Daniel Strauss reports:\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  -aligned North Carolina congressman Madison Cawthorn now has a Democratic opponent.\par \par \uc2 Commissioner J asmine Beach-Ferrara of Buncombe County, an ordained minister and executive director of the Campaign for Southern Equality, kicked off her campaign Wednesday morning with a biographic video:\par \par \uc2 enltrRep. Madison Cawthorn has a Democratic challenger ???? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/IjJnft3QCh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/IjJnft3QCh}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Daniel Strauss (@DanielStrauss4) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/DanielStrauss4/status/1367110124519759884?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 3, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 Cawthorn is the 25-year old congressman from North Carolina who is closely aligned with the pro-Trump wing of the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 A number of women have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2021/03/01/politics/madison-cawthorn-sexual-misconduct-allegations/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 come forward}}}\uc2  to accuse Cawthorn of sexual misconduct. The Washington Post also published {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2021/02/27/making-madison-cawthorn-how-falsehoods-helped-propel-career-new-pro-trump-star-far-right/?arc404=true"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a deep dive}}}\uc2  into the falsehoods that helped drive Cawthorn\u8217\'20\'19s ascent.\par \par \uc2 Beach-Ferrara has assembled a team of veteran strategists in her bid. Jeffrey Liszt of ALG Research is doing polling. Clare Gannon and Mattis Goldman of Three Point Media are handling media. Eric Goldman of Break Something Strategies is doing digital. Nadia Garnett and Adnaan Muslim of Deliver Strategies are doing the mail. Eric Hyers, a veteran Democratic campaign manager, is also advising the campaign.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.50pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Former top White House physician made sexual comments and drank on the job \u8211\'20\'13 report\par \par \uc2 The Department of Defense has this morning issued a scathing review of Representative \uc2 Ronny Jackson\uc2  during his time serving as the top White House physician. The report was obtained by CNN, and conclude that he made \u8220\'20\'1Csexual and denigrating\u8221\'20\'1D comments about a female subordinate, drank alcohol while on a presidential trip, and took prescription-strength sleeping medication that prompted colleagues to be worried about his ability to provide proper care. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://edition.cnn.com/2021/03/02/politics/ronny-jackson-dod-inspector-general-report/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 CNN writes}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 After interviewing 78 witnesses and reviewing a host of White House documents, investigators concluded that Jackson, who achieved the rank of Rear Admiral, failed to treat his subordinates with dignity and respect, engaged in inappropriate conduct involving the use of alcohol during two incidents and used sleeping medication during an overseas trip that raised concerns about his ability to provide medical care to the President and other top officials, according to the report.\par \par \uc2 The report also notes that the investigation into Jackson \u8220\'20\'1Cwas limited in scope and unproductive\u8221\'20\'1D as White House counsel under Trump insisted on being present at all interviews of current White House Medical Unit employees, which had a \u8220\'20\'1Cpotential chilling effect\u8221\'20\'1D on the probe.\par \par \uc2 CNN analysis showed that of the 60 witnesses interviewed by the Defense Department IG about the command climate under Jackson, only 13 had positive comments, while 38 spoke about \u8220\'20\'1Cunprofessional behavior, intimidation and poor treatment of subordinates.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Events detailed in the report include \u8220\'20\'1CJackson \u8216\'20\'18pounding\u8217\'20\'19 on the door of his female subordinate\u8217\'20\'19s room\u8221\'20\'1D after he had been drinking on a presidential trip. On another occasion he was witnessed drinking a beer while he was serving as the physician to the President and in charge of providing medical care for a presidential trip, despite regulations prohibiting him.\par \par \uc2 Read more here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://edition.cnn.com/2021/03/02/politics/ronny-jackson-dod-inspector-general-report/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 CNN \u8211\'20\'13 Rep Ronny Jackson made sexual comments, drank alcohol and took Ambien while working as White House physician, Pentagon watchdog finds}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.04pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.46pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Parler, the social network used to spread right-wing violent threats, drops case against \uc2 Amazon\par \par \uc2 Parler, the social media app popular among American right-wing users, has dropped its case against \uc2 Amazon\uc2  for cutting off its web-hosting services, court documents from late last night showed.\par \par \uc2 Reuters report that the app went dark in January as many service providers pulled back support, accusing it of failing to police violent content related to the attack on the US Capitol, the nation\u8217\'20\'19s legislative seat, by followers of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . \uc2 Google\uc2  removed the application from its Play Store and Apple from App Store.\par \par \uc2 Parler sued \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , accusing it of making an illegal, politically motivated decision to shut it down to benefit \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A US judge rejected its demand that \uc2 Amazon\uc2  restore services for the platform later in January. A month later, Parler re-launched its services online and said the new platform was built on \u8220\'20\'1Csustainable, independent technology\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2021/jan/19/parler-website-partially-returns-with-support-from-russian-owned-technology-firm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 albeit apparently from Russia}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has said that Parler ignored repeated warnings to effectively moderate the growth of violent content on its website, including calls to assassinate prominent Democratic politicians, leading business executives and members of the media.\par \par \uc2 Parler, however, has said there was no evidence apart from anecdotes in the press that it had a role in inciting the riots in US Capitol and argued that it was unfair to deprive millions of law-abiding Americans a platform for free speech. It should be noted that the \u8220\'20\'1Canecdote in the press\u8221\'20\'1D that Parler refers to were the media quoting what Parler had allowed to be published on its website \u8211\'20\'13 as document, for example, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theverge.com/2021/1/13/22228675/amazon-parler-takedown-violent-threats-moderation-content-free-speech"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.31pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Sam Levin reports for us from Los Angeles on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/02/rodney-king-lapd-police-30-years-later"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the legacy of the brutal beating of Rodney King by the LAPD}}}\uc2 , which happened thirty years ago:\par \par \uc2 LAPD in recent years has faced scrutiny from a new wave of activists who have organized against police killings of civilians, discriminatory arrests and traffic stops, harassment and surveillance of Black and Latino residents, and militarized responses to protests. Arguing previous reforms have done little to curb the department\u8217\'20\'19s brazenness, they are calling for sweeping change: take away power, and funding, from LAPD.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLAPD is still corrupt and violent and brutal,\u8221\'20\'1D said Melina Abdullah, co-founder of Black Lives Matter LA. \u8220\'20\'1CSince Rodney King, LAPD has just gotten slicker. Very little has changed, other than they\u8217\'20\'19ve gotten better at PR.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The calls to defund LAPD gained traction during last year\u8217\'20\'19s uprisings after the killings of George Floyd and Breonna Taylor \u8211\'20\'13 and activists are launching a new campaign to build on that momentum. \u8220\'20\'1CWe can reimagine public safety by divesting from police and freeing up those dollars to invest in the things that actually bring about safe communities,\u8221\'20\'1D said Abdullah.\par \par \uc2 Recent data has repeatedly shown that LAPD officers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2019/oct/08/los-angeles-police-stop-search-black-latino"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 stop and search}}}\uc2  Black and Latino residents at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/sep/30/los-angeles-police-citations-black-residents"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 significantly higher rates}}}\uc2  than white residents. In the last two years, officers have been accused of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/california/story/2020-07-14/california-bars-police-from-using-lapd-records-in-gang-database-as-scandal-widens"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 falsely labeling civilians}}}\uc2  as gang members in databases, citing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/california/story/2020-10-02/three-more-lapd-officers-charged-with-falsifying-information-in-gang-labeling-scandal"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fabricated information}}}\uc2 . Last year, the LA Times {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/california/story/2020-06-11/lapd-violated-protocols-for-batons-and-less-lethal-bullets-injuring-many-protesters"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 found}}}\uc2  that the police department appeared to have repeatedly violated its own rules when it used force against protesters, in some cases causing significant injury. And in the last month, LAPD has been twice forced to apologize \u8211\'20\'13 first after officers allegedly circulated an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/feb/15/lapd-investigation-george-floyd-image"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 offensive meme mocking George Floyd}}}\uc2 , then after the chief admitted he {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/california/story/2021-02-23/citing-data-error-lapd-acknowledges-hundreds-more-south-l-a-stops-than-acknowledged"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 gave hugely inaccurate data to the LA Times}}}\uc2  that falsely suggested a major decline in controversial traffic stops.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere have been reforms on paper, but if you ask community members if anything has changed, they say nothing has, really,\u8221\'20\'1D said Andr\u233\'00\'E9s Dae Keun Kwon, a lawyer with the ACLU of Southern California. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s the same old disproportionate stops, targeting, harassment, brutalizing and killing.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe were hopeful that this kind of confirmation of our experiences would mean that there would be justice and meaningful change,\u8221\'20\'1D Abdullah, of BLM, said of the King footage. Instead, \u8220\'20\'1Cwe saw police double down on violence and brutality \u8230\'20\'26 telling the world, \u8216\'20\'18Don\u8217\'20\'19t believe your lying eyes.\u8217\'20\'19 It reminded us that the truth wasn\u8217\'20\'19t really important to a system that put targets on the backs of Black people.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Read more of Sam Levin\u8217\'20\'19s report here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/02/rodney-king-lapd-police-30-years-later"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Rodney King \u8211\'20\'13 30 years after brutal beating, activists say LAPD \u8216\'20\'18still corrupt and violent\u8217\'20\'19}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/02/rodney-king-lapd-police-30-years-later"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Rodney King: 30 years after brutal beating, activists say LAPD 'still corrupt and violent'}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.24pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Alexi McCammond at Axios reports on the push by the progressive wing of the Democratic party to get itself in a position to challenge a host of centrist incumbents during the next primary season. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/progressives-challenge-steny-hoyer-house-democrats-a6549bbd-13e6-416b-93e4-0c413a9ebda8.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 She writes}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 Progressive Democrats, including two who are Black, are lining up to challenge House majority leader \uc2 Steny Hoyer\uc2  even before Maryland sets the date for its 2022 primaries.\par \par \uc2 Recent progressive victories for Reps Cori Bush in Missouri and \uc2 Jamaal Bowman\uc2  in New York, plus the country\u8217\'20\'19s changing demographics and post-#MeToo and George Floyd eras, are giving organizers and candidates new hope that the political landscape is changing and rewarding diversity.\par \par \uc2 Progressives feel a special urgency to get their policies passed into law, given Democrats control the House, Senate and White House. That\u8217\'20\'19s propelling their unity against those party leaders and members they believe aren\u8217\'20\'19t fighting for the policies like activists. Hoyer is just one member of the Democratic old guard who\u8217\'20\'19s being targeted early by the left flank, with a renewed focus on race and gender.\par \par \uc2 Senate majority leader Chuck Schumer is up for reelection in 2022 and faces constant speculation about whether a progressive like Rep Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez will mount a primary challenge against him. In Virginia, Gov Ralph Northam is term-limited from seeking reelection, but his seat is being eyed by Jennifer Carroll Foy, a Black mother of twins and public defender. And Even before the recent allegations of sexual harassment against New York Gov Andrew Cuomo, progressives were quietly looking to Attorney General Letitia James as a formidable primary challenger.\par \par \uc2 Read more here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/progressives-challenge-steny-hoyer-house-democrats-a6549bbd-13e6-416b-93e4-0c413a9ebda8.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Axios \u8211\'20\'13 Progressives ready challenge to Democratic old guard}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.18pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Jeff Sessions has expressed regret that migrant children were separated from their parents at the US-Mexico border, reports Ted Hesson for Reuters.\par \par \uc2 As attorney general, Sessions was the cabinet official responsible for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Czero-tolerance\u8221\'20\'1D border strategy and a \uc2 US Department of Justice\uc2  internal watchdog report released in January said Sessions\u8217\'20\'19 office was \u8220\'20\'1Ca driving force\u8221\'20\'1D behind the administration\u8217\'20\'19s decision to refer families for criminal prosecution, and that Sessions himself was aware it could lead to family separations.\par \par \uc2 The policy charged parents with federal immigration offences and sent them to jails, while children were labeled \u8220\'20\'1Cunaccompanied\u8221\'20\'1D and placed in shelters.\par \par \uc2 Between April and June 2018, nearly 3,000 migrant children were separated from their parents at the border while another 1,000 children were separated from their parents during a pilot program in 2017.\par \par \uc2 Efforts are still ongoing in court to locate the parents of more than 500 separated children.\par \par \uc2 Despite the Trump administration only making a belated and ineffective effort to reunite families, Sessions expressed regret for the separations:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt was unfortunate, very unfortunate, that somehow the government was not able to manage those children in a way that they could be reunited properly,\u8221\'20\'1D said Sessions. \u8220\'20\'1CIt turned out to be more of a problem than I think any of us imagined it would be.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Launched in April 2018, Trump\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Czero-tolerance\u8221\'20\'1D policy {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2018/jun/18/us-immigration-border-families-separated-children-kirstjen-nielsen"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 provoked widespread criticism}}}\uc2 , leading the Republican president to effectively reverse it months later.\par \par \uc2 Within days of taking office, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , created a task force in February to reunite families still separated by the policy, calling it a \u8220\'20\'1Cmoral and national shame\u8221\'20\'1D and a \u8220\'20\'1Cstain\u8221\'20\'1D on the reputation of the United States.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.27pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.09pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Moustafa Bayoumi writes for us this morning on what he says is a new low \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2021/mar/03/ice-reached-a-new-low-using-utility-bills-to-hunt-undocumented-immigrants"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 using utility bills to hunt undocumented immigrants}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 The startling truth is that signing up for even basic utilities in this country has turned into a gamble for many people, particularly undocumented immigrants. Last week, the Washington Post {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/technology/2021/02/26/ice-private-utility-data/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 revealed}}}\uc2  that \uc2 US Immigration and Customs Enforcement\uc2  (Ice) has paid tens of millions of dollars {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.usaspending.gov/award/CONT_AWD_HSCEMD17F00008_7012_GS02F026DA_4732"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 since 2017}}}\uc2  for access to a private database that contains more than \u8220\'20\'1C400m names, addresses and service records from more than 80 utility companies covering all the staples of modern life, including water, gas and electricity, and phone, internet and cable TV\u8221\'20\'1D. The information has been mined by Ice, the Post reported, for immigration surveillance and enforcement operations.\par \par \uc2 Neither Ice nor any other federal agency should have unfettered access to this data. In fact, there are strict protocols and regulations that determine how the federal government can gather your information and when it can infringe on your privacy, much of this is codified in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.justice.gov/opcl/privacy-act-1974"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Privacy Act of 1974}}}\uc2 , as the Post notes. So how are federal agencies like Ice getting around these legal safeguards, which would otherwise prevent them from scooping up such data on their own and without a court order? Simple. They just buy it. With taxpayer money.\par \par \uc2 Ice paid {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.usaspending.gov/award/CONT_AWD_HSCEMD17F00008_7012_GS02F026DA_4732"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 almost $21m}}}\uc2  for access to a database called Clear, which is owned by the multinational media conglomerate \uc2 Thomson Reuters\uc2 . Clear is reported to contain billions of your records, including employment and housing information, credit reports, criminal histories, vehicle registrations and data from utility companies in all 50 states, Washington DC, Puerto Rico, Guam and the US Virgin Islands. It\u8217\'20\'19s also updated daily.\par \par \uc2 This isn\u8217\'20\'19t just {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/books/2019/oct/04/shoshana-zuboff-surveillance-capitalism-assault-human-automomy-digital-privacy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 surveillance capitalism}}}\uc2 . It\u8217\'20\'19s worse. The main idea behind surveillance capitalism is that we, the world\u8217\'20\'19s internet users and smartphone aficionados, have been persuaded to give up the wealth of our personal information in meager exchange for convenient access to big data\u8217\'20\'19s apps and platforms. But what Ice has been doing is different. The marriage of government and surveillance capitalism reveals yet another depth to our contemporary, pixelated nightmare.\par \par \uc2 Because the power of the government is so immense, the union of government might with surveillance capitalism should worry every single one of us. \uc2 Facebook\uc2  may want to know everything about your shopping and surfing habits, but perhaps the worst it can do to you individually is put you in a metaphorical \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.socialmediatoday.com/social-networks/terrygreen3309/2015-06-06/how-stay-out-facebook-jail"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Facebook jail}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D. Governments, needless to say, can send you to a real prison.\par \par \uc2 Read more here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2021/mar/03/ice-reached-a-new-low-using-utility-bills-to-hunt-undocumented-immigrants"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Moustafa Bayoumi \u8211\'20\'13 Ice reached a new low: using utility bills to hunt undocumented immigrants}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2021/mar/03/ice-reached-a-new-low-using-utility-bills-to-hunt-undocumented-immigrants"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ice reached a new low: using utility bills to hunt undocumented immigrants | Moustafa Bayoumi}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.01pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Overnight, Giovanni Russonello\u8217\'20\'19s On Politics newsletter for the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  had {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/02/us/politics/voting-rights-supreme-court-georgia-arizona.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a focus on voting rights restrictions}}}\uc2  that Republicans are attempting to impose across the US in the wake of their November election defeat.\par \par \uc2 There are over 250 bills pending in 43 states that would restrict access to voting. He spoke to Wendy Weiser, director of the Democracy Program at the Brennan Center for Justice at \uc2 NYU\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s law school, and she had this to say about the efforts:\par \par \uc2 [There are] seven times the number of restrictive voting bills we saw at the same time last year. So it is a dramatic spike in the push to restrict access to voting. It\u8217\'20\'19s not brand-new this year, it wasn\u8217\'20\'19t invented by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , but it was certainly supercharged by his regressive attack on our voting systems.\par \par \uc2 Many of these bills are fueled by the same rhetoric and grievances that were driving the challenges to the 2020 election. In addition to expressly referencing the big lie about widespread voter fraud and that Trump actually won the election, they\u8217\'20\'19re targeting the methods of voting that the Trump campaign was complaining about. So, for example, the single biggest subject of regressive voter legislation in this session \u8212\'20\'14 roughly half the bills \u8212\'20\'14 is mail voting.\par \par \uc2 That is new this year. We\u8217\'20\'19ve been tracking efforts to restrict access to voting for a very long time, and absentee voting has not been the subject of legislative attack before. It was the politicization of that issue in the 2020 election, principally by the Trump campaign and allies, that I think helped elevate that issue to a grievance level that would cause it to be the subject of legislative attack.\par \par \uc2 The Democratic party is fighting back with an attempt at federal legislation of its own \u8211\'20\'13 with the For the People Act. As Weiser explains it:\par \par \uc2 It would create a baseline level of voter access rules that every American could rely on for federal elections. So, for example, in many states we\u8217\'20\'19re seeing attempts to eliminate no-excuse absentee voting. This would require all states to offer no-excuse absentee voting. Every state would then offer that best practice of voting access, and it would no longer be manipulated, election by election, by state legislators to target voters they don\u8217\'20\'19t like.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.52pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Sarah Marsh reports for Reuters that eighty House Democrats urged \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  on Tuesday to repeal \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Ccruel\u8221\'20\'1D sanctions on Cuba and renew engagement, an early sign of support in Congress for easing a clamp-down on the country.\par \par \uc2 In a letter to Biden seen by Reuters they urged the Democratic president to sign an executive order \u8220\'20\'1Cwithout delay\u8221\'20\'1D to end restrictions on travel and remittances, noting that well over half of Cubans depend on the latter.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWith the stroke of a pen, you can assist struggling Cuban families and promote a more constructive approach,\u8221\'20\'1D they said.\par \par \uc2 The letter was led by lawmakers Bobby Rush, Gwen Moore, Barbara Lee and \uc2 Steve Cohen\uc2 , long-time supporters of engagement with Cuba. Signers also included the leaders of the influential House of Representatives Foreign Affairs, Financial Services and Appropriations committees.\par \par \uc2 Biden vowed during his campaign to reverse policy shifts by the Republican Trump that \u8220\'20\'1Chave inflicted harm on the Cuban people and done nothing to advance democracy and human rights.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s tightening of the decades-old US trade embargo on Cuba has inflicted further pain on its ailing state-run economy, contributing to worsening shortages of food and medicine. But Biden has not yet indicated whether he will fully revert to the historic detente initiated by Democratic former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  when Biden was vice president.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis letter, signed by a number of key powerbrokers in the House of Representatives, will help empower US foreign policy officials in the Biden administration who seek to rebuild what Trump destroyed - a constructive, productive and civil approach toward Cuba and its people,\u8221\'20\'1D said Peter Kornbluh, co-author of \u8220\'20\'1CBack Channel to Cuba\u8221\'20\'1D and senior analyst at the National Security Archive.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration took more than 200 initiatives to tighten the decades-old US trade embargo on Cuba over four years, citing concerns about a lack of democracy and Havana\u8217\'20\'19s support for Venezuela\u8217\'20\'19s socialist government.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 1.27pm GMT\par \par \uc2 12.42pm GMT\par \par \uc2 In the last year, \uc2 Facebook\uc2  adjusted some of the most fundamental rules about what gets posted on its platform, halting {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2021/feb/04/facebook-groups-misinformation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 algorithmic recommendations}}}\uc2  of political groups, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2021/feb/08/facebook-bans-vaccine-misinformation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 banning lies}}}\uc2  about vaccines and removing a number of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2019/may/02/facebook-ban-alex-jones-milo-yiannopoulos"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 high-profile figures}}}\uc2  for spreading misinformation and hate \u8211\'20\'13 including \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But researchers say the social media platform is not enforcing those policies as effectively when it comes to misinformation in Spanish \u8211\'20\'13 a blind spot that may prove deadly as health lies spread through the most vulnerable populations during the global vaccine effort.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPrior to the election, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/facebook"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Facebook}}}\uc2  was rolling out new enforcement actions and policy updates week after week,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Carmen Scurato\uc2 , a senior policy counsel at the civil rights group Free Press who studies Spanish-language misinformation. \u8220\'20\'1CBut what we are observing is that those enforcement actions don\u8217\'20\'19t seem to be replicated in Spanish.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAlthough before the election we saw \uc2 Facebook\uc2  make an effort to take down some disinformation, we did not see that same effort on Spanish content,\u8221\'20\'1D echoed Jacobo Licona, the disinformation research lead for Equis Labs, a polling firm focused on Latino voters. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s disappointing, and could have a negative impact on Spanish-speaking communities.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 There are more than {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.census.gov/newsroom/facts-for-features/2019/hispanic-heritage-month.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 59 million Spanish speakers}}}\uc2  in the US, and the demographic is growing on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 . According to \uc2 Facebook\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s own market research data, more than 70% of Latinos who use social media prefer \uc2 Facebook\uc2  over other online platforms.\par \par \uc2 But Spanish-language content is less often and less quickly moderated for misinformation and violence than English content, research shows. While 70% of misinformation in English on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  ends up flagged with warning labels, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://secure.avaaz.org/campaign/en/facebook_coronavirus_misinformation/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 just 30% of comparable misinformation in Spanish}}}\uc2  is flagged, according to a study from the human rights non-profit \uc2 Avaaz\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Facebook\uc2  is leaving out the millions of people who speak Spanish at home by failing to apply its community standards equally,\u8221\'20\'1D Scurato said. \u8220\'20\'1CIf you say you are making efforts on your platform for the safety and health of all of us, that has to also include the Latinx community.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Read more of Kari Paul\u8217\'20\'19s report from San Francisco here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2021/mar/03/facebook-spanish-language-misinformation-covid-19-election"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Facebook has a blind spot\u8217\'20\'19: why Spanish-language misinformation is flourishing}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2021/mar/03/facebook-spanish-language-misinformation-covid-19-election"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 'Facebook has a blind spot': why Spanish-language misinformation is flourishing }}}\par \par \uc2 12.37pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Congressional attempt to access \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tax records set to continue into summer\par \par \uc2 Kyle Cheney {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2021/03/02/trump-financial-records-472959"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reports for Politico}}}\uc2  that the battle to get access to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s tax records is set to run and run\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 Congressional investigators fighting to access former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s financial records are planning for a lengthy battle that stretches deep into 2021, according to a proposed legal schedule unveiled by House counsel Doug Letter on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 In a filing on Tuesday with the federal District Court in DC, Letter revealed that the House Oversight Committee had reissued a subpoena for Trump\u8217\'20\'19s accounting firm, Mazars USA, late last month. And he outlined a briefing and argument schedule \u8212\'20\'14 already agreed to by Trump\u8217\'20\'19s legal team \u8212\'20\'14 that would carry the case into June, with a decision unlikely before mid-summer. Any appeals by either Trump or the House could extend that timeline much further.\par \par \uc2 Letter\u8217\'20\'19s filing is the first window into the House\u8217\'20\'19s plans for a lengthy slog in a battle that has already stretched two years in federal courts. Democrats say they need access to Trump\u8217\'20\'19s records \u8212\'20\'14 which they suspect will show improper foreign ties and potential conflicts of interest \u8212\'20\'14 in order to craft financial disclosure legislation to restrain future administrations. But the effort also has renewed relevance after Trump reemerged over the weekend, vowing vengeance upon his political adversaries and pledging to retain his stranglehold on GOP politics.\par \par \uc2 Under Letter\u8217\'20\'19s schedule, Trump\u8217\'20\'19s first brief would be due 5 April, with the committee\u8217\'20\'19s reply a month later. Additional briefs would be filed on 19 May and 2 June. Oral argument would be scheduled \u8220\'20\'1Cas soon as practicable\u8221\'20\'1D after that.\par \par \uc2 Read more here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2021/03/02/trump-financial-records-472959"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Politico \u8211\'20\'13 House fight for Trump\u8217\'20\'19s financial records poised to stretch into the summer}}}\par \par \uc2 Updated at 1.29pm GMT\par \par \uc2 12.12pm GMT\par \par \uc2 In not unsurprising news, this morning the Kremlin has played down the impact of sanctions imposed by the United States and the \uc2 European Union\uc2  over Moscow\u8217\'20\'19s treatment of opposition politician \uc2 Alexei Navalny\uc2 , but said it would retaliate with reciprocal measures.\par \par \uc2 In President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s most direct challenge yet to the Kremlin, the US imposed sanctions to punish Russia for what it described as Moscow\u8217\'20\'19s attempt to poison Navalny with a nerve agent last year.\par \par \uc2 Navalny, 44, fell ill on a flight in Siberia in August and was airlifted to Germany, where doctors concluded he had been poisoned with a nerve agent. The Kremlin has denied any role in his illness and said it has seen no proof he was poisoned.\par \par \uc2 Washington on Tuesday imposed sanctions against seven senior Russian officials and on 14 entities. Kremlin spokesman Dmitry Peskov said Moscow would hit back in a way that best served its interests.\par \par \uc2 Reuters report that Peskov told reporters \u8220\'20\'1COf course it\u8217\'20\'19s impossible not to apply the principle of reciprocity. We consider such decisions to be absurd, unjustified and most importantly, they have no effect or meaning,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe can only regret this and express our bewilderment.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 However, Peskov said the US sanctions would have no effect on the senior officials targeted because they are not allowed to travel outside Russia, own property abroad or hold foreign bank accounts anyway because of the sensitivity of their positions.\par \par \uc2 11.52am GMT\par \par \uc2 US militia group draws members from military and police, website leak shows\par \par \uc2 A Guardian investigation of a website leak from the American Patriots Three Percent shows the anti-government militia group have recruited a network across the United States that includes current and former military members, police and border patrol agents.\par \par \uc2 But the leak also demonstrates how the radical group has recruited from a broad swath of Americans, not just military and law enforcement. Members include both men and women, of ages ranging from their 20s to their 70s, doing jobs from medical physics to dental hygiene and living in all parts of the country.\par \par \uc2 Experts say the revelations of the broad scope of the movement\u8217\'20\'19s membership shows the mainstreaming of the radical politics of militia and so-called \u8220\'20\'1CPatriot Movement\u8221\'20\'1D groups during the Trump era and beyond.\par \par \uc2 There has been a particular focus on the militia movement after the 6 January attack on the Capitol in Washington DC, in which a rampaging pro-Trump mob included militia members and others from far-right organizations.\par \par \uc2 According to members who spoke to \uc2 the Guardian\uc2 , the website from which the list was leaked was set up by national leaders of Patriot Movement group, which is affiliated with the broader Three Percenter movement.\par \par \uc2 Names, phone numbers and even photographs of members were obtained by activists who then posted the data to an internet archiving site, and \uc2 the Guardian\uc2  cross-referenced these with public records and other published materials.\par \par \uc2 One of the activists who discovered the leak, whose name has been withheld due to safety concerns, said that the Wordpress site\u8217\'20\'19s poorly configured membership plugin left those details exposed to public view. Additional materials seen by \uc2 the Guardian\uc2  confirm that claim, and show that the materials were obtained by a simple search technique.\par \par \uc2 Many of the members revealed by the leak have extensive armed forces experience, including some who are still serving in branches of the US military.\par \par \uc2 Read more of Jason Wilson\u8217\'20\'19s analysis here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/03/us-militia-membership-military-police-american-patriot-three-percenter-website-leak"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US militia group draws members from military and police, website leak shows}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/03/us-militia-membership-military-police-american-patriot-three-percenter-website-leak"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US militia group draws members from military and police, website leak shows}}}\par \par \uc2 11.18am GMT\par \par \uc2 Also in the Senate today, the nomination of \uc2 Xavier Becerra\uc2  faces a key vote in the Finance committee. It\u8217\'20\'19s a test, too, for national groups opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , trying to deny a president who favors \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights his choice to run the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ricardo Alonso-Zaldivar and Thomas Beaumont write for Associated Press that Becerra is paying a price for defending, as California attorney general, some of the nation\u8217\'20\'19s most liberal laws and policies on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 During the 2020 election, about 6 in 10 voters said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in most or all cases, according to VoteCast, an in-depth survey of the US electorate conducted by NORC at the University of Chicago for the Associated Press. Roughly the same percentage of Republicans said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should usually be legal, the survey showed.\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless lawmakers in 19 state legislatures have introduced almost 50 bills this year to ban most or all abortions, according to the nonpartisan \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 . In South Carolina, Republican \uc2 Gov Henry McMaster\uc2  signed a measure banning most abortions, though it was almost immediately suspended by a federal judge.\par \par \uc2 Becerra was appointed California attorney general in 2017. He sued the Trump administration over its restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , although his office says that only four of the 124 lawsuits Becerra filed against the previous administration dealt with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , birth control or conscience rights \u8212\'20\'14 key issues for religious conservatives. Becerra went all the way to the \uc2 US Supreme Court\uc2  to defend a California law that required crisis pregnancy centers to provide information about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8212\'20\'14 and lost.\par \par \uc2 His legal advocacy grated on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat I just see is his getting involved in way too many \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cases,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Kristen Day\uc2 , executive director of Democrats for Life of America. \u8220\'20\'1CHe just made it part of his foundation. Yes, the laws were bad in California, but he has an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  agenda.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Republican \uc2 John Thune\uc2 , echoed those views. \u8220\'20\'1CIt does seem like as attorney general you spent an inordinate amount of time and effort suing pro-life organizations,\u8221\'20\'1D he said, questioning Becerra recently. \u8220\'20\'1CIf confirmed, how do you assure us?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Sen Steve Daines, told Becerra that \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve got serious concerns with the radical views that you\u8217\'20\'19ve taken in the past on the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D And \uc2 Sen Ben Sasse\uc2  accused Becerra of \u8220\'20\'1Ctargeting religious liberty\u8221\'20\'1D when he sued the Trump administration over its rules giving employers with religious or moral objections more leeway to opt out of covering birth control.\par \par \uc2 11.14am GMT\par \par \uc2 Senate expected to take up Biden\u8217\'20\'19s $1.9tn coronavirus relief package today\par \par \uc2 The Senate is expected to take up \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s $1.9tn coronavirus relief package today, with fellow Democrats seeking to advance key priorities and jettison aspects that have drawn unflattering scrutiny.\par \par \uc2 The bill would pay for vaccines and medical supplies, boost jobless assistance and send a new round of emergency financial aid to households, small businesses and state and local governments. Democrats aim to get it to Biden to sign into law before 14 March, when some current benefits expire.\par \par \uc2 With Republican cooperation unlikely, Democrats who narrowly control the chamber need to stick together to pass Biden\u8217\'20\'19s top legislative priority. That will require them to sort out a welter of competing ideas as they seek to advance the bill, which passed the House last Saturday. We already know that the proposal to raise the federal minimum wage to $15 per hour has to be excised.\par \par \uc2 Also on the chopping block, reports Andy Sullivan for Reuters, is $1.5 million for a bridge connecting Canada and New York state, which Republicans have derided, though Democratic Senate majority leader Chuck Schumer says it was requested by the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 It is not clear whether Democrats will keep another project that has drawn Republican ire - funding for a subway expansion in California\u8217\'20\'19s Silicon Valley.\par \par \uc2 Democrats have shown no interest in dropping another partisan sticking point - $350 billion in aid for state and local governments, which face rising costs and uncertain tax revenues because of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Democratic Senator Joe Manchin is pushing to scale back enhanced unemployment benefits to $300 per week from $400. Lawmakers may also opt to discontinue those benefits if unemployment in a given state drops below a certain level, according to a Democratic aide.\par \par \uc2 Democrats also may tighten income qualifications for $1,400 direct payments, so they are more targeted toward lower-income households, an aide said.\par \par \uc2 The Senate could vote on the bill by the end of the week. The House would then have to sign off on the changes before Congress could send it on to Biden to sign into law.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 1.30pm GMT\par \par \uc2 10.56am GMT\par \par \uc2 With Covid case numbers continuing to fall, and the promise of the vaccine roll-out, states are beginning to list some of the restrictions that have been imposed during the pandemic. Greg Abbott has announced that Texas is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/02/texas-governor-ends-mask-mandate-and-declares-it"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 flinging open businesses to full capacity}}}\uc2  while simultaneously ending its highly politicized mask mandate. Julie Bosman and Lucy Tompkins for the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  report on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/02/us/coronavirus-reopening-texas.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 other openings around the US}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 In Chicago, tens of thousands of children returned to public school this week, while snow-covered parks and playgrounds around the city that have been shuttered since last March were opened. Mississippi ended its mask mandate, too. Restaurants in Massachusetts were allowed to operate without capacity limits, and South Carolina erased its limits on large gatherings. San Francisco announced that indoor dining, museums, movie theaters and gyms could reopen on a limited basis.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s despite the warnings from \uc2 Dr Rochelle Walensky\uc2 , director of the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. She said on Monday \u8220\'20\'1CI know people are tired; they want to get back to life, to normal. But we\u8217\'20\'19re not there yet.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As Tompkins and Bosman write:\par \par \uc2 Though national statistics have improved drastically since January, they have plateaued in the last week or so, and the US is still reporting more than 65,000 new cases a day on average \u8212\'20\'14 comparable to the peak of last summer\u8217\'20\'19s surge. The country is averaging more than 2,000 deaths per day, though deaths are a lagging indicator because it can take weeks after being infected with the coronavirus to die from it.\par \par \uc2 New, more contagious variants of the virus are circulating in the country, with the potential to push case counts upward again. Testing has fallen 30 percent in recent weeks, leaving experts worried about how quickly new outbreaks will be known. And millions of Americans are still waiting to be vaccinated \u8212\'20\'14 including workers in restaurants, which are now open in vast numbers across the country.\par \par \uc2 Read more here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/02/us/coronavirus-reopening-texas.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New York Times \u8211\'20\'13 Texas drops its virus restrictions as a wave of reopenings takes hold}}}\par \par \uc2 10.40am GMT\par \par \uc2 Yesterday \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said the country was on track to have enough vaccines for every adult in the country by the end of May. In a sideswipe at former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Covid efforts, the president said \u8220\'20\'1CWhen we came into office, the prior administration had contracted for not nearly enough vaccine to cover adults in America. We rectified that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s a clip from his address on coronavirus yesterday.\par \par \uc2 The White House Covid response team also emphasised the way that they have pulled forward the ambition to vaccinate every adult in the US \u8211\'20\'13 in gif form.\par \par \uc2 About three weeks ago, President Biden said we'd have enough vaccine supply for all adult Americans by the end of July.\par \par \uc2 Through partnership with vaccine manufacturers and our efforts to ramp up its production, we will now have enough vaccine supply by the end of May. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/lgrpkXYKIr"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/lgrpkXYKIr}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 White House COVID-19 Response Team (@WHCOVIDResponse) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/WHCOVIDResponse/status/1366872336998289414?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 2, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 According to CDC data, at least 50 million adults in the US have now received at least their first shot of a Covid vaccine \u8211\'20\'13 including Dolly Parton, who {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/music/2021/mar/03/dolly-parton-gets-vaccinated-with-moderna-jab-she-helped-fund"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 got a job of the Moderna vaccine that she helped fund}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Dolly gets a dose of her own medicine. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/VUMChealth?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @VUMChealth}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/38kJrDzLqC"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/38kJrDzLqC}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Dolly Parton (@DollyParton) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/DollyParton/status/1366866210852323328?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 2, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 10.35am GMT\par \par \uc2 Welcome to today\u8217\'20\'19s live coverage of US politics. Here\u8217\'20\'19s a catch-up on where we are, and a little of what we can expect to see today:\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/02/us-vaccines-coronavirus-biden-teachers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the US expects to have enough coronavirus vaccines for all adults by the end of May}}}\uc2 , two months earlier than anticipated.\par \par \uc2 * Biden also announced he would be using the powers of the federal government to direct all states to prioritize vaccinating teachers and said the federal government would provide the doses directly through its pharmacy program.\par \par \uc2 * His administration said that the drugmaker Merck would help produce \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s newly approved shot.\par \par \uc2 * There were 53,544 new Covid cases, and 1,819 further deaths in the US. Hospitalization levels are now down to 46,388 across the country.\par \par \uc2 * Republican governors have seized on the declining numbers to start re-opening the economy. Despite experts warning a premature lifting of restrictions could spark another surge in infection, Texas\u8217\'20\'19 Greg Abbot announced he was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/02/texas-governor-ends-mask-mandate-and-declares-it"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rescinding the state\u8217\'20\'19s mask mandate}}}\uc2  and business would be able to operate at 100% from next week.\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/02/neera-tanden-withdraws-nomination-biden-cabinet"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Neera Tanden withdrew as a Cabinet nominee}}}\uc2  after facing opposition. In an ironic demonstration of \u8220\'20\'1Ccancel culture\u8221\'20\'1D in action, Republican senators cited Tanden\u8217\'20\'19s tweets in opposing her nomination for director of the budget office.\par \par \uc2 * The Senate Finance committee will vote today on the nomination of \uc2 Xavier Becerra\uc2  to run the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 * The Senate homeland security committee and the Senate rules and administration committee will hold their second joint hearing on the Capitol insurrection at 10am ET (1500 GMT), with testimony from senior DHS and \uc2 FBI\uc2  officials.\par \par \uc2 * The White House Covid response team will give their latest press briefing at 11am, and \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  gives a briefing at 12.30pm.\par \par \uc2 * At 1.45pm President Biden holds a bipartisan meeting on cancer in the Oval Office which Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  will also attend. At 5pm Biden takes part in a virtual event with the House Democratic Caucus.\par \par \uc2 2021-03-03T10:35:38Z2021-03-05T11:00:34Z{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://media.guim.co.uk/f98b93e558f334faea3384d55579ce2cb81c47df/0_300_4500_2701/500.jpgLetitia"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://media.guim.co.uk/f98b93e558f334faea3384d55579ce2cb81c47df/0_300_4500_2701/500.jpgLetitia}}}\uc2  James at an Asian American Foundation \u8216\'20\'18Rise Up Against Asian Hate\u8217\'20\'19 rally in New York on 27 February.Maj Gen William J Walker testifies before two Senate committees.Shalanda Young testifies in a confirmation hearing before the \uc2 Senate Committee on the Budget.FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  testifies during a hearing before the Senate judiciary committee.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210303eh33001gv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
