\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Catholics in New Orleans and St Louis told to avoid \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  vaccine\b0\par\par\uc2 Jessica Glenza and Martin Pengelly \par \uc2 910 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 March 2021\par 22:18 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 11\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Religious leaders in two cities say use of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -derived cells in development means believers should prefer \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  and Moderna\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nolacatholic.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 archdiocese of New Orleans}}}\uc2 , as well as Roman Catholic leaders in St Louis, Missouri, have told local Catholics to avoid the \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s single-shot Covid-19 vaccine, because its early development used \u8220\'20\'1Cmorally compromised cell lines created from two abortions\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The leadership in New Orleans said two other vaccines in use in the US, made by \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 -BioNTech and Moderna, were acceptable despite having been developed with \u8220\'20\'1Csome lab testing that utilised the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -derived cell line\u8221\'20\'1D. The archdiocese made the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nolacatholic.org/news/a-statement-regarding-the-janssen-johnson-johnson-covid-19-vaccine"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announcement}}}\uc2  on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The statement put the archdiocese at odds with Pope Francis. In December, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2020/dec/22/vatican-says-getting-covid-vaccine-morally-acceptable"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Vatican said}}}\uc2  it was \u8220\'20\'1Cmorally acceptable to receive Covid-19 vaccines that have used cell lines from aborted foetuses in their research and production process\u8221\'20\'1D, as the use of such vaccines \u8220\'20\'1Cdoes not constitute formal cooperation with the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from which the cells used in production of the vaccines derive\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The archdiocese of St Louis on Tuesday, meanwhile, encouraged Catholics to seek out the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  or Moderna vaccines and avoid the \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  version if possible. Like the New Orleans archdiocese statement, the St Louis statement called the \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  vaccine \u8220\'20\'1Cmorally compromised\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 However, the St Louis statement stressed that Catholics can get that vaccine \u8220\'20\'1Cin good conscience if no other alternative is available\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Cells derived from an elective \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Netherlands in the 1970s are commonly used in medical research. Last October, it was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2020/oct/08/trump-covid-drug-developed-using-cells-derived-from-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 widely reported}}}\uc2  that an experimental Covid-19 therapy given to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , Regeneron, was developed using such cells.\par \par \uc2 Like most Republicans, the then president had courted anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups and moved to restrict use of fetal tissue in research. Fetal tissue research has led to a number of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ama-assn.org/delivering-care/ethics/research-using-human-fetal-tissue"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 important medical advances}}}\uc2 , especially in vaccine development.\par \par \uc2 Cell lines derived from aborted tissue were used in the development of the polio, chickenpox, hepatitis A and shingles vaccines. \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s vaccine subsidiary Janssen used {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sciencemag.org/news/2020/06/abortion-opponents-protest-covid-19-vaccines-use-fetal-cells"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 PER.C6 cells}}}\uc2 , a proprietary cell line derived from aborted tissue in 1985.\par \par \uc2 The New Orleans archdiocese said it maintained \u8220\'20\'1Cthat the decision to receive the Covid-19 vaccine remains one of individual conscience in consultation with one\u8217\'20\'19s healthcare provider. We also maintain that in no way does the church\u8217\'20\'19s position diminish the wrongdoing of those who decided to use cell lines from abortions to make vaccines.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn doing so, we advise that if the Moderna or \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  vaccine is available, Catholics should choose to receive either of those vaccines rather than to receive the new \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  vaccine because of its extensive use of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -derived cell lines.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The diocese\u8217\'20\'19s comments come as the US undertakes national distribution of three coronavirus vaccines authorized for emergency use by drugs regulators. Vaccine hesitancy has been a major concern throughout the pandemic, as vaccines need to reach a vast majority of adults to protect those ineligible to receive vaccines, such as children under 16 and the immune-compromised.\par \par \uc2 More than 28.6 million people in the US have been infected by the coronavirus and 515,000 killed since the pandemic began.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Dr Anthony Fauci\uc2 , the nation\u8217\'20\'19s top infectious disease expert and chief medical adviser to the president, are both Catholic. Asked about vaccine efficacy, Fauci {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/feb/28/covid-vaccines-us-anthony-fauci"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told NBC}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1CAll three of them are really quite good, and people should take the one that\u8217\'20\'19s most available to them. People need to get vaccinated as quickly and as expeditiously as possible, and if I would go to a place where they had \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2 , I would have no hesitancy whatsoever to take it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups and Catholic leaders in the US and Canada have raised ethical objections to the origin of cell lines throughout the pandemic. In June, the US Conference of Catholic Bishops wrote a letter urging the Trump administration to \u8220\'20\'1Cincentivize\u8221\'20\'1D the development of vaccines that do not use such cell lines.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is critically important that Americans have access to a vaccine that is produced ethically,\u8221\'20\'1D the letter {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sciencemag.org/news/2020/06/abortion-opponents-protest-covid-19-vaccines-use-fetal-cells"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CNo American should be forced to choose between being vaccinated against this potentially deadly virus and violating his or her conscience.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 New Orleans is not the only diocese to engage in anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rhetoric. In January, Joseph Strickland, bishop of Tyler, Texas and \u8220\'20\'1Chost of The Bishop Strickland Hour on VMP Radio\u8221\'20\'1D, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Bishopoftyler/status/1352468902928932870"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tweeted}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1CAll the political posturing on vaccines is truly disgusting.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe fact remains that ANY vaccine available today involves using murdered children before they could even be born. I renew my pledge \u8230\'20\'26 I will not extend my life by USING murdered children. This is evil WAKE UP!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Arthur Caplan, a bioethicist at the \uc2 New York University School of Medicine\uc2 , told {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sciencemag.org/news/2020/06/abortion-opponents-protest-covid-19-vaccines-use-fetal-cells"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Science magazine}}}\uc2  in June: \u8220\'20\'1CThere are better ways to win the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars than telling people not to use a vaccine. These are long-over abortions. These cells are decades old, and even major religious leaders like the pope have acknowledged that for the greater good it\u8217\'20\'19s not worth the symbolism to put the community at risk.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/ng-interactive/2020/sep/21/coronavirus-covid-19-map-us-cases-deaths-state-by-state"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 According to Johns Hopkins University}}}\uc2 , by Tuesday morning Louisiana had recorded 430,504 Covid-19 cases and 9,628 deaths. According to one {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/graphics/2020/health/covid-vaccine-states-distribution-doses/?itid=lk_inline_manual_27"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 vaccinations tracker}}}\uc2 , nearly 700,000 people had received at least one shot in the state.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210302eh32001rx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
